&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Research tries to create new information, while surveillance regularly collects data in a systematic way.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Epidemiological research aims to &lt;strong&gt;generate new knowledge&lt;/strong&gt; by using scientific methods to explore causes, risk factors, and associations related to diseases. It focuses on answering a &lt;strong&gt;specific research question&lt;/strong&gt; and has a &lt;strong&gt;defined beginning and end&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Surveillance is the &lt;strong&gt;continuous, systematic collection, analysis, and interpretation of health data&lt;/strong&gt; with the goal of &lt;strong&gt;monitoring disease trends, detecting outbreaks, and guiding public health actions&lt;/strong&gt;. It is performed routinely rather than to test a hypothesis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The key difference lies in the purpose: research focuses on &lt;strong&gt;knowledge creation and hypothesis testing&lt;/strong&gt;, whereas surveillance focuses on &lt;strong&gt;ongoing monitoring and timely decision-making&lt;/strong&gt; to protect public health.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Research often involves &lt;strong&gt;sampling, comparisons, and statistical inference&lt;/strong&gt;, while surveillance includes &lt;strong&gt;real-time data flow from health facilities, laboratories, or registries&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Surveillance is done only when there is no outbreak, and research is done only during an outbreak.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Surveillance operates during both &lt;strong&gt;outbreak and non-outbreak periods&lt;/strong&gt;. Research is not limited to outbreaks but can be conducted at any time to explore health-related questions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Surveillance always looks at past events, while research always looks toward the future.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Surveillance may collect &lt;strong&gt;real-time or prospective data&lt;/strong&gt;, not only past data. Research can be &lt;strong&gt;retrospective or prospective,&lt;/strong&gt; depending on the study design.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Research always needs ethical permission, but surveillance usually does not.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Ethical approval is required in &lt;strong&gt;most types of research&lt;/strong&gt;, but modern public health policies also require &lt;strong&gt;ethical oversight and data protection measures for surveillance&lt;/strong&gt;, especially when involving personal health information.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Epidemiological research is conducted to &lt;strong&gt;discover new scientific evidence&lt;/strong&gt;, whereas surveillance focuses on &lt;strongongoing a=&quot;&quot; health=&quot;&quot; in=&quot;&quot; monitoring=&quot;&quot; of=&quot;&quot; population=&quot;&quot; strong=&quot;&quot; systematic=&quot;&quot;&gt;. Both are crucial for public health, but their &lt;strong&gt;purpose, duration, and methodology are fundamentally different&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/strongongoing&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Checking the size of the pupils and how they react to light.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In the first 24 hours after a thrombotic stroke (CVA), the most important nursing assessment is &lt;strong&gt;neurological status monitoring&lt;/strong&gt;, because the client is at high risk for &lt;strong&gt;brain edema, increased intracranial pressure (ICP), and worsening of the stroke&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Assessing &lt;strong&gt;pupil size and reaction to light&lt;/strong&gt; is a critical component of the &lt;strong&gt;neurological examination&lt;/strong&gt;. Changes such as &lt;strong&gt;unequal pupils (anisocoria), non-reactive pupils, or dilated pupils&lt;/strong&gt; may indicate &lt;strong&gt;brain herniation, expanding hematoma, or increased ICP&lt;/strong&gt;, which require immediate medical intervention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Neurological assessments&amp;mdash;including level of consciousness, Glasgow Coma Scale, motor response, and pupillary reaction&amp;mdash;are performed &lt;strong&gt;frequently in the acute phase&lt;/strong&gt; to detect early signs of deterioration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Prompt recognition of neurological decline allows &lt;strong&gt;rapid treatment interventions&lt;/strong&gt;, improving survival and preventing permanent brain damage.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Measuring cholesterol level&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Cholesterol testing is important for &lt;strong&gt;long-term management and prevention of future strokes&lt;/strong&gt;, but it is &lt;strong&gt;not a priority in the first 24 hours&lt;/strong&gt; when rapid neurological assessment is crucial.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Listening to bowel sounds&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Bowel assessment helps rule out ileus or constipation, but it does &lt;strong&gt;not provide immediate information about neurological deterioration&lt;/strong&gt;, making it a lower priority during the acute phase of a stroke.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Doing an echocardiogram&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: An echocardiogram may be done later to identify &lt;strong&gt;cardiac sources of emboli&lt;/strong&gt;, but it is &lt;strong&gt;not an urgent first-day assessment&lt;/strong&gt; when neurological monitoring is the priority.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In the first 24 hours following a thrombotic stroke, the &lt;strong&gt;highest priority is monitoring neurological status&lt;/strong&gt;, especially &lt;strong&gt;pupil size and reaction to light&lt;/strong&gt;, because it provides the &lt;strong&gt;earliest indication of increasing intracranial pressure and stroke progression&lt;/strong&gt;, allowing immediate and life-saving interventions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Turning the patient into the prone (face-down) position.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A patient with severe acute pancreatitis who is on a ventilator with &lt;strong&gt;FiO₂ 100% and PEEP 12 cm H₂O&lt;/strong&gt;, and has &lt;strong&gt;bilateral lung infiltrates with poor oxygenation&lt;/strong&gt;, is showing signs highly suggestive of &lt;strong&gt;Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome (ARDS)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In ARDS, the lungs become &lt;strong&gt;stiff and filled with inflammatory fluid&lt;/strong&gt;, causing very low oxygen levels even with high ventilatory support.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The first intervention to improve oxygenation is &lt;strong&gt;prone positioning&lt;/strong&gt; because it increases &lt;strong&gt;ventilation&amp;ndash;perfusion (V/Q) matching, reduces lung compression by the heart and abdomen, and improves alveolar recruitment&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Proning has been proven to &lt;strong&gt;significantly increase oxygen saturation, improve lung recruitment, and reduce mortality&lt;/strong&gt; in severe ARDS when combined with adequate PEEP.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Lowering the PEEP to reduce the risk of lung pressure injury&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Reducing PEEP would &lt;strong&gt;worsen oxygenation&lt;/strong&gt; by causing alveoli to &lt;strong&gt;collapse further&lt;/strong&gt;. In ARDS, maintaining moderate&amp;ndash;high PEEP is necessary to keep alveoli open.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Increasing the tidal volume for better oxygen delivery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Increasing tidal volume can lead to &lt;strong&gt;ventilator-induced lung injury (VILI)&lt;/strong&gt;. In ARDS, a &lt;strong&gt;lung-protective ventilation strategy&lt;/strong&gt; with low tidal volume is recommended.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Immediately reducing FiO₂ to prevent oxygen toxicity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Oxygen toxicity becomes a concern over time, but at this moment &lt;strong&gt;oxygenation is critically low&lt;/strong&gt;, so reducing FiO₂ is not appropriate until gas exchange improves.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In a mechanically ventilated patient with severe ARDS and refractory hypoxemia, &lt;strong&gt;prone positioning is the immediate and most effective intervention&lt;/strong&gt; to improve oxygenation before considering adjustments to ventilator settings or oxygen levels. It enhances &lt;strong&gt;lung recruitment, gas exchange, and survival outcomes&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 90 mL/hr.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The patient requires &lt;strong&gt;dopamine at 15 mcg/min&lt;/strong&gt;, which must be converted and matched with the concentration available in the IV bag to calculate the correct infusion rate.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;First convert micrograms to milligrams since the solution is labeled in mg:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;15 mcg/min = 0.015 mg/min&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Convert dose per minute to dose per hour:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;0.015 mg &amp;times; 60 min = 0.9 mg/hr&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Determine concentration of the IV solution:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;5 mg dopamine / 500 mL = 0.01 mg/mL&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Calculate infusion rate using the formula:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;Required dose (mg/hr) &amp;divide; Concentration (mg/mL) = mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;0.9 mg/hr &amp;divide; 0.01 mg/mL = 90 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, setting the IV pump to &lt;strong&gt;90 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt; will deliver the correct dopamine dose of &lt;strong&gt;15 mcg/min&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;180 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This rate would &lt;strong&gt;double the required dose&lt;/strong&gt;, resulting in excessive dopamine administration and risking tachycardia, arrhythmias, or hypertension.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;19 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This value delivers &lt;strong&gt;far below the prescribed dose&lt;/strong&gt;, causing inadequate perfusion and poor hemodynamic response.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;9 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This setting provides only &lt;strong&gt;10% of the required dose&lt;/strong&gt;, which would offer no therapeutic effect in shock management.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To safely deliver dopamine at &lt;strong&gt;15 mcg/min&lt;/strong&gt; from a solution of &lt;strong&gt;5 mg in 500 mL D5W&lt;/strong&gt;, the correct IV pump setting is &lt;strong&gt;90 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;. Accurate calculation ensures effective support of cardiac output while preventing medication overdose or underdose.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Verbigeration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Verbigeration refers to the &lt;strong&gt;meaningless, purposeless, and involuntary repetition of words or phrases&lt;/strong&gt;, commonly seen in psychiatric conditions such as schizophrenia and severe psychosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this case, the patient keeps repeating &amp;ldquo;I want to go home... home... home,&amp;rdquo; which shows &lt;strong&gt;continuous repetition without communication intent&lt;/strong&gt;, matching the definition of verbigeration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The repetition happens even though the phrase no longer adds new information or contributes to interaction, highlighting &lt;strong&gt;disorganized thought processes&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Echopraxia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Refers to the &lt;strong&gt;involuntary imitation of another person&amp;rsquo;s movements or gestures&lt;/strong&gt;, not speech, so it does not match the scenario.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Perseveration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Involves &lt;strong&gt;repeating a response (word, phrase, or activity) when no longer appropriate to the situation&lt;/strong&gt;, often driven by cognitive impairment rather than purposeless repetition. It is different from verbigeration, which is &lt;strong&gt;automatic and meaningless speech repetition&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Echolalia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Echolalia refers to the &lt;strong&gt;imitation or echoing of another person&amp;rsquo;s spoken words&lt;/strong&gt;. In the question, the patient is repeating their own words, not someone else&amp;rsquo;s, so it is not echolalia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The repetitive and purposeless recurrence of the same words by the psychiatric patient aligns with &lt;strong&gt;verbigeration&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Intradermal (ID)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Mantoux test (tuberculin skin test) is administered by the &lt;strong&gt;intradermal (ID) route&lt;/strong&gt;. A small amount of purified protein derivative (PPD) is injected &lt;strong&gt;into the dermis of the skin&lt;/strong&gt;, usually on the inner surface of the forearm.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The test works by triggering a &lt;strong&gt;delayed hypersensitivity immune response&lt;/strong&gt; in individuals previously exposed to Mycobacterium tuberculosis. If immune cells recognize the antigen, a &lt;strong&gt;raised swelling (induration)&lt;/strong&gt; will develop at the injection site after 48&amp;ndash;72 hours.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct technique is essential: a &lt;strong&gt;5&amp;ndash;15&amp;deg; angle insertion&lt;/strong&gt; is used to ensure the solution remains within the dermis, forming a &lt;strong&gt;small wheal (6&amp;ndash;10 mm)&lt;/strong&gt; immediately after injection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Only &lt;strong&gt;intradermal administration&lt;/strong&gt; ensures accurate reading of induration and proper detection of cell-mediated immune response, which is the purpose of the test.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Intramuscular (IM)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Medication is injected deep into muscle tissue in IM route. This delivers systemic absorption and would &lt;strong&gt;not produce a localized skin reaction&lt;/strong&gt;, therefore not suitable for Mantoux test.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Intravenous (IV)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This route introduces medication directly into the bloodstream. IV injection would &lt;strong&gt;not assess delayed hypersensitivity&lt;/strong&gt; and would be unsafe for tuberculin testing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Subcutaneous (SC)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Subcutaneous injection goes into the fat tissue below the skin. Injecting PPD here would cause &lt;strong&gt;diffusion into deeper tissues&lt;/strong&gt;, giving &lt;strong&gt;incorrect or unreadable results&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Mantoux (tuberculin skin) test must be given &lt;strong&gt;intradermally&lt;/strong&gt; to produce a visible wheal and allow proper interpretation of induration after 48&amp;ndash;72 hours. Other routes such as IM, IV, or SC would &lt;strong&gt;invalidate the test and make interpretation impossible&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: A liquid oral dose of 5 drops&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Under India&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)&lt;/strong&gt;, the rotavirus vaccine is administered as a &lt;strong&gt;liquid oral vaccine&lt;/strong&gt;, not by injection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The recommended dose is &lt;strong&gt;5 drops given directly into the infant&amp;rsquo;s mouth&lt;/strong&gt; using the provided dropper.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The vaccine works by providing &lt;strong&gt;local immunity in the gut&lt;/strong&gt;, where rotavirus infection causes severe diarrhea, dehydration, and hospitalization in infants.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Since the goal is to stimulate immunity within the gastrointestinal tract, &lt;strong&gt;oral administration is the most effective method&lt;/strong&gt; for rotavirus vaccine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A subcutaneous shot of 1 mL in the upper arm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Rotavirus vaccine is &lt;strong&gt;not given as an injectable vaccine&lt;/strong&gt;. SC shots are used for vaccines such as MMR and JE, not rotavirus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An intramuscular injection of 0.5 mL into the thigh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: IM administration is used for vaccines such as pentavalent, PCV, and IPV. &lt;strong&gt;Rotavirus vaccine is not administered intramuscularly.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A dry (lyophilized) oral dose of 2 drops&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: No dry (lyophilized) rotavirus formulation is used in UIP. The recommended formulation is &lt;strong&gt;liquid 5-drop oral dose&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In India&amp;rsquo;s UIP, the rotavirus vaccine is correctly administered as a &lt;strong&gt;liquid oral dose of 5 drops&lt;/strong&gt;. It is not given by SC or IM injections, and no lyophilized oral form is used in the national immunization schedule.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Uterine rupture&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The clinical picture strongly indicates a &lt;strong&gt;uterine rupture&lt;/strong&gt;, which is an obstetric emergency. In a twin labour, sudden maternal deterioration along with abnormal fetal heart patterns suggests a catastrophic event affecting both mother and babies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Key features pointing to uterine rupture include: &lt;strong&gt;sudden loss of fetal heart sounds of the second baby&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;low fetal heart rate of the first baby&lt;/strong&gt;, and the mother showing a &lt;strong&gt;very weak pulse, shock, and cardiovascular collapse&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;During a rupture, the uterus tears, causing &lt;strong&gt;massive internal bleeding, severe maternal shock, and disruption of fetal oxygen supply&lt;/strong&gt; to both babies. This explains why &lt;strong&gt;both fetal heart rates are critically affected simultaneously&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Uterine rupture is more commonly seen during labour, especially in &lt;strong&gt;multiparity, obstructed labour, induction/augmentation with oxytocin, and previous cesarean section&lt;/strong&gt;, but it can also occur with twin pregnancy because of uterine overdistension.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Placental abruption&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Abruptio placenta causes fetal distress and maternal bleeding, but &lt;strong&gt;it would affect mainly one placenta at a time&lt;/strong&gt;. It is less likely to cause &lt;strong&gt;the sudden disappearance of second fetal heart sound plus shock at the same moment&lt;/strong&gt; in twin labour.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cord prolapse of the second baby&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Cord prolapse leads to &lt;strong&gt;acute fetal bradycardia or absent fetal heart sounds&lt;/strong&gt; in only &lt;strong&gt;one fetus&lt;/strong&gt;, but it does &lt;strong&gt;not cause sudden maternal shock with a weak pulse&lt;/strong&gt;. The mother&amp;#39;s deterioration points to heavy internal hemorrhage, not cord prolapse.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Vasa previa bleeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Vasa previa causes &lt;strong&gt;bleeding from fetal vessels&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to rapid fetal death of one baby but &lt;strong&gt;does not cause immediate maternal shock&lt;/strong&gt;. The dramatic maternal collapse in this case does not fit vasa previa.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The combination of &lt;strong&gt;sudden maternal shock, very weak pulse, fetal distress of the first baby, and complete loss of heart sounds of the second baby durin &lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Tele-MANAS mental health support&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The helpline number &lt;strong&gt;14416 (or 1800-891-4416)&lt;/strong&gt; in India is dedicated to the &lt;strong&gt;Tele-MANAS mental health support service&lt;/strong&gt;, launched by the Ministry of Health &amp;amp; Family Welfare.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This service provides &lt;strong&gt;24&amp;times;7 free mental health counselling&lt;/strong&gt;, psychological first aid, and connects callers to &lt;strong&gt;trained mental health professionals, psychologists, and psychiatrists&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The helpline is available in multiple regional languages and aims to support individuals dealing with &lt;strong&gt;stress, anxiety, depression, suicidal thoughts, emotional distress, substance-use issues, and other mental health concerns.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Tele-MANAS ensures &lt;strong&gt;confidential, accessible, and immediate assistance&lt;/strong&gt; for mental health needs across India through tele-counselling and referral pathways when required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;National emergency helpline (112)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: 112 is India&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;all-in-one emergency number&lt;/strong&gt; like 911 (police, fire, ambulance), but it is &lt;strong&gt;not related to mental health tele-counselling services.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;e-Sanjeevani telemedicine service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: e-Sanjeevani is a digital platform for &lt;strong&gt;online medical OPD consultations&lt;/strong&gt;, but it does not operate via helpline number 14416.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;KIRAN mental health rehabilitation helpline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The KIRAN helpline is another mental health service, but its number is &lt;strong&gt;1800-599-0019&lt;/strong&gt;, not 14416. KIRAN focuses more on &lt;strong&gt;mental health rehabilitation and disability support&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The national helpline number &lt;strong&gt;14416 (or 1800-891-4416)&lt;/strong&gt; is officially assigned to &lt;strong&gt;Tele-MANAS&lt;/strong&gt;, which provides &lt;strong&gt;round-the-clock mental health counselling and support across India&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: After the birth of a Rh-positive baby, the recommended dose is only 100 &amp;mu;g.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Anti-D immunoglobulin is given to &lt;strong&gt;Rh-negative mothers&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent &lt;strong&gt;Rh isoimmunization&lt;/strong&gt; after exposure to Rh-positive fetal blood.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After delivery of a &lt;strong&gt;Rh-positive baby&lt;/strong&gt;, the standard recommended dose is &lt;strong&gt;300 &amp;mu;g (1500 IU)&lt;/strong&gt; within 72 hours of birth, not 100 &amp;mu;g.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This dose protects against approximately &lt;strong&gt;30 mL of fetal whole blood (or 15 mL of fetal RBCs)&lt;/strong&gt; entering the maternal circulation, which is common during childbirth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, stating that only &lt;strong&gt;100 &amp;mu;g is recommended postpartum&lt;/strong&gt; is incorrect because this amount is insufficient for postpartum fetomaternal hemorrhage prevention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A 300 &amp;mu;g dose can protect against about 30 mL of fetal red blood cells&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This statement is &lt;strong&gt;correct&lt;/strong&gt;. A 300 &amp;mu;g dose protects against &lt;strong&gt;30 mL fetal blood&lt;/strong&gt; exposure, the standard protection required after delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In early-pregnancy abortion, a 50 &amp;mu;g dose is used&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is &lt;strong&gt;correct&lt;/strong&gt;. For abortions or bleeding events &lt;strong&gt;before 12 weeks&lt;/strong&gt;, a smaller dose of &lt;strong&gt;50 &amp;mu;g&lt;/strong&gt; is adequate, as fetal blood volume is much lower.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After a first-trimester pregnancy loss, a 300 &amp;mu;g dose may be required in some cases&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is &lt;strong&gt;correct&lt;/strong&gt; when &lt;strong&gt;fetomaternal hemorrhage is suspected to be higher&lt;/strong&gt;, such as in surgical procedures or repeated bleeding episodes.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The incorrect statement is the one recommending &lt;strong&gt;only 100 &amp;mu;g&lt;/strong&gt; after the delivery of a Rh-positive infant. The appropriate standard postpartum dose remains &lt;strong&gt;300 &amp;mu;g&lt;/strong&gt; to effectively prevent Rh alloimmunization.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Platelet level of 18,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A platelet count of &lt;strong&gt;18,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/strong&gt; represents a condition of &lt;strong&gt;severe thrombocytopenia&lt;/strong&gt;, which is a medical emergency in a chemotherapy patient because of the &lt;strong&gt;high risk of spontaneous internal bleeding&lt;/strong&gt;, including intracranial hemorrhage and gastrointestinal bleeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;When platelets fall below &lt;strong&gt;20,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/strong&gt;, clotting ability is critically impaired and the patient must be treated immediately with &lt;strong&gt;bleeding precautions and possible platelet transfusion&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thrombocytopenia is a common toxicity of chemotherapy as the bone marrow is suppressed and the body cannot produce enough platelets, making timely intervention crucial for survival.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Neutrophil count of 2,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This indicates &lt;strong&gt;mild neutropenia&lt;/strong&gt; (normal 2,500&amp;ndash;6,000/&amp;mu;L), but not a life-threatening condition. Serious infection risk increases when neutrophils fall below &lt;strong&gt;1,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/strong&gt;, and becomes critical below &lt;strong&gt;500/&amp;mu;L&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;WBC count of 4,500/&amp;mu;L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This value is at the &lt;strong&gt;lower end of normal range&lt;/strong&gt; (4,500&amp;ndash;11,000/&amp;mu;L) and is &lt;strong&gt;not an emergency&lt;/strong&gt;, even though WBC may drop during chemotherapy.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hemoglobin of 8.2 g/dL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This indicates &lt;strong&gt;moderate anemia&lt;/strong&gt;, which is common with chemotherapy and may require iron, erythropoietin therapy, or blood transfusion but is &lt;strong&gt;not immediately life-threatening&lt;/strong&gt; like severe thrombocytopenia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Among the given laboratory results, a platelet count of &lt;strong&gt;18,000/&amp;mu;L&lt;/strong&gt; is the most urgent finding in a chemotherapy patient because it poses a &lt;strong&gt;high and immediate risk of spontaneous internal hemorrhage&lt;/strong&gt;, requiring &lt;strong&gt;emergent medical intervention&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Watch the child closely for repeated swallowing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After a tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, the greatest postoperative danger is &lt;strong&gt;hemorrhage from the surgical site&lt;/strong&gt;. Bleeding may not always be visible in the mouth and can be &lt;strong&gt;silently swallowed by the child&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the most important safety instruction for the mother is to &lt;strong&gt;observe the child for repeated or continuous swallowing&lt;/strong&gt;, especially when the child is sleepy or lying down.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Repeated swallowing is an early and subtle sign of &lt;strong&gt;active bleeding&lt;/strong&gt; and requires &lt;strong&gt;immediate medical attention&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent shock and airway obstruction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Let the child return to all activities right away&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is &lt;strong&gt;incorrect&lt;/strong&gt;. Children must avoid strenuous activities for at least &lt;strong&gt;1&amp;ndash;2 weeks&lt;/strong&gt; because physical exertion increases the risk of postoperative bleeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Offer spicy or hard foods soon after surgery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: &lt;strong&gt;Incorrect&lt;/strong&gt; and unsafe. Spicy, acidic, or hard foods irritate the throat and can &lt;strong&gt;dislodge the healing clot&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to bleeding. The child should receive &lt;strong&gt;soft, cool, non-irritating foods&lt;/strong&gt; instead.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Encourage strong gargling to clean the throat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Gargling can &lt;strong&gt;loosen the surgical scab&lt;/strong&gt; and provoke &lt;strong&gt;bleeding or trauma&lt;/strong&gt; to the healing tissue. Mouth rinsing should be gentle if recommended at all.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy, the &lt;strong&gt;most critical nursing instruction&lt;/strong&gt; is to &lt;strong&gt;watch for repeated swallowing&lt;/strong&gt; as it indicates possible postoperative bleeding. Prompt detection can prevent life-threatening complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Helping with controlled cord traction&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;During the &lt;strong&gt;third stage of labour&lt;/strong&gt;, controlled cord traction (CCT) is done to assist in the &lt;strong&gt;safe delivery of the placenta&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this procedure, the birth attendant gives &lt;strong&gt;gentle downward traction on the umbilical cord&lt;/strong&gt; while the &lt;strong&gt;other hand presses gently on the mother&amp;rsquo;s lower abdomen (suprapubic area)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The suprapubic hand &lt;strong&gt;supports and stabilizes the uterus&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent &lt;strong&gt;uterine inversion&lt;/strong&gt; as the placenta is being delivered.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This is a key component of &lt;strong&gt;Active Management of the Third Stage of Labour (AMTSL)&lt;/strong&gt; to reduce postpartum hemorrhage and ensure controlled placental separation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Giving perineal massage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is done in the &lt;strong&gt;second stage of labour&lt;/strong&gt; on the perineum (not on the abdomen) to prevent perineal tears. It is not related to placental delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Providing fundal pressure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Fundal pressure involves applying pressure on the &lt;strong&gt;top of the uterus&lt;/strong&gt; to assist birth of the baby, not for placental delivery. The picture shows pressure on the &lt;strong&gt;lower abdomen (suprapubic area)&lt;/strong&gt;, not the fundus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Massaging the uterus after the placenta is out&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Uterine massage is performed &lt;strong&gt;after the placenta is delivered&lt;/strong&gt; to ensure firm contraction and prevent hemorrhage, not during placental extraction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Pressing gently on the lower abdomen during the third stage of labour is part of &lt;strong&gt;controlled cord traction&lt;/strong&gt;, providing suprapubic counterpressure so the placenta can be delivered safely without the risk of uterine inversion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: The reading will appear higher than the actual value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Using a blood pressure cuff that is &lt;strong&gt;too small&lt;/strong&gt; for the patient&amp;#39;s arm results in &lt;strong&gt;excessive compression of the artery&lt;/strong&gt; during inflation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because of this excessive pressure, the sphygmomanometer will &lt;strong&gt;detect the pulse only at a higher pressure&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to a falsely elevated reading.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This error applies to both systolic and diastolic values, making the recorded blood pressure appear &lt;strong&gt;higher than the patient&amp;rsquo;s true blood pressure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Proper cuff size should cover around &lt;strong&gt;40% of arm circumference in width&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;80% of arm circumference in length&lt;/strong&gt; to obtain accurate readings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The reading will be lower than the true value&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A &lt;strong&gt;large cuff&lt;/strong&gt; (not a small one) produces falsely low BP readings by not applying enough pressure to fully occlude the artery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The reading will remain correct&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is incorrect because cuff size directly affects blood pressure accuracy; using a cuff too small never produces an accurate reading.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Blood pressure cannot be measured&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Blood pressure can still be measured&amp;mdash;but it will be &lt;strong&gt;inaccurate and falsely high&lt;/strong&gt;, not impossible.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A cuff that is &lt;strong&gt;too small&lt;/strong&gt; for the patient&amp;rsquo;s arm will make the blood pressure reading appear &lt;strong&gt;higher than the actual value&lt;/strong&gt;. Correct cuff size is essential for obtaining an accurate measurement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div style=&quot;font-family: Arial, sans-serif; line-height: 1.6;&quot;&gt;
&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE)&lt;/strong&gt; is a standardized tool used to assess a patient&amp;rsquo;s cognitive function, including orientation, memory, attention, calculation, language, and visuospatial skills. The total possible score is &lt;strong&gt;30&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;According to the &lt;strong&gt;provided MMSE interpretation table&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;24&amp;ndash;30&lt;/strong&gt; : No cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;19&amp;ndash;23&lt;/strong&gt; : Mild cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;10&amp;ndash;18&lt;/strong&gt; : Moderate cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&amp;lt; 9&lt;/strong&gt; : Severe cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p&gt;In this case, the patient has scored &lt;strong&gt;19 out of 30&lt;/strong&gt;. This score lies at the lower limit of the range &lt;strong&gt;19&amp;ndash;23&lt;/strong&gt;, which corresponds to &lt;strong&gt;mild cognitive impairment&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;Although the score is close to the cutoff for moderate impairment, classification must be done strictly according to the defined ranges in the reference table. Therefore, the correct interpretation is &lt;strong&gt;mild cognitive impairment&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hence, the correct answer is: Option 1 &amp;ndash; Mild cognitive impairment.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Conscious sedation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The term &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;conscious anesthesia&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt; refers to &lt;strong&gt;conscious sedation&lt;/strong&gt;, a type of anesthesia where the patient remains &lt;strong&gt;awake but relaxed&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In conscious sedation, the patient is able to &lt;strong&gt;breathe independently, respond to verbal commands, and maintain protective reflexes&lt;/strong&gt;, while pain and anxiety are reduced.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This technique is commonly used for &lt;strong&gt;endoscopy, minor surgeries, dental procedures, and diagnostic procedures&lt;/strong&gt; where full loss of consciousness is not required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Medications are given to produce &lt;strong&gt;sedation + analgesia + anxiolysis&lt;/strong&gt; without inducing complete unconsciousness like general anesthesia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;General anesthesia causing deep sleep&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: General anesthesia results in &lt;strong&gt;complete loss of consciousness and protective reflexes&lt;/strong&gt;. This is &lt;strong&gt;not conscious anesthesia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Local anesthesia only&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Local anesthesia blocks sensation in a specific area but the term &lt;strong&gt;conscious anesthesia does not refer to only local anesthesia&lt;/strong&gt; and may include sedation medications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Regional anesthesia such as spinal or epidural&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Regional anesthesia blocks sensation to a larger body region. Although the patient may remain awake, this is &lt;strong&gt;not defined as conscious anesthesia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&amp;ldquo;Conscious anesthesia&amp;rdquo; means the patient is &lt;strong&gt;awake but relaxed and pain-free&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;a state achieved through &lt;strong&gt;conscious sedation&lt;/strong&gt;, not through general, local, or regional anesthesia alone.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p data-end=&quot;45&quot; data-start=&quot;0&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;19&quot; data-start=&quot;0&quot;&gt;Correct Answer:&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;strong data-end=&quot;45&quot; data-start=&quot;20&quot;&gt;4) Esophageal atresia&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p data-end=&quot;80&quot; data-start=&quot;47&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;80&quot; data-start=&quot;51&quot;&gt;Rationale&amp;nbsp;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li data-end=&quot;150&quot; data-start=&quot;83&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;116&quot; data-start=&quot;83&quot;&gt;Single umbilical artery (SUA)&lt;/strong&gt; is most commonly associated with:

	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li data-end=&quot;174&quot; data-start=&quot;155&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;174&quot; data-start=&quot;155&quot;&gt;Renal anomalies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li data-end=&quot;204&quot; data-start=&quot;179&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;204&quot; data-start=&quot;179&quot;&gt;Genitourinary defects&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li data-end=&quot;270&quot; data-start=&quot;209&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;245&quot; data-start=&quot;209&quot;&gt;Lower gastrointestinal anomalies&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., imperforate anus)&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li data-end=&quot;296&quot; data-start=&quot;275&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;296&quot; data-start=&quot;275&quot;&gt;Cardiac anomalies&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li data-end=&quot;437&quot; data-start=&quot;299&quot;&gt;These associations are related to &lt;strong data-end=&quot;369&quot; data-start=&quot;333&quot;&gt;mesodermal developmental defects&lt;/strong&gt;, which frequently coexist with abnormal umbilical artery formation.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li data-end=&quot;549&quot; data-start=&quot;441&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;463&quot; data-start=&quot;441&quot;&gt;Esophageal atresia&lt;/strong&gt; is a &lt;strong data-end=&quot;488&quot; data-start=&quot;469&quot;&gt;foregut anomaly&lt;/strong&gt; (endodermal origin) and is &lt;strong data-end=&quot;539&quot; data-start=&quot;516&quot;&gt;not commonly linked&lt;/strong&gt; with SUA.&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li data-end=&quot;643&quot; data-start=&quot;552&quot;&gt;Therefore, among the given options, &lt;strong data-end=&quot;642&quot; data-start=&quot;588&quot;&gt;esophageal atresia is the least likely association&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p data-end=&quot;667&quot; data-start=&quot;645&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;667&quot; data-start=&quot;649&quot;&gt;Exam one-liner&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;blockquote data-end=&quot;774&quot; data-is-last-node=&quot;&quot; data-is-only-node=&quot;&quot; data-start=&quot;668&quot;&gt;
&lt;p data-end=&quot;774&quot; data-is-last-node=&quot;&quot; data-start=&quot;670&quot;&gt;&lt;strong data-end=&quot;774&quot; data-is-last-node=&quot;&quot; data-start=&quot;670&quot;&gt;SUA &amp;rarr; Renal + GU + Lower GI + Cardiac anomalies; foregut anomalies (esophagus) are least associated.&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/p&gt;
&lt;/blockquote&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Blood glucose levels&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hydrocortisone is a &lt;strong&gt;corticosteroid&lt;/strong&gt;, and one of its rapid effects after IV administration is &lt;strong&gt;increasing blood glucose levels&lt;/strong&gt; by promoting gluconeogenesis and decreasing glucose utilization in tissues.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because of this, patients &amp;mdash; especially those with &lt;strong&gt;diabetes, prediabetes, stress hyperglycemia, or critical illness&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;mdash; are at risk of developing &lt;strong&gt;acute hyperglycemia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the &lt;strong&gt;priority nursing action immediately after giving IV hydrocortisone is to check the patient&amp;#39;s blood glucose levels&lt;/strong&gt; to detect and manage steroid-induced hyperglycemia early.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Restrict the patient&amp;rsquo;s fluid intake&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hydrocortisone can cause fluid retention, but &lt;strong&gt;fluid restriction is not routinely recommended&lt;/strong&gt; immediately after administration and is not a first priority.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Encourage a diet high in salt&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hydrocortisone already causes &lt;strong&gt;sodium and water retention&lt;/strong&gt;. Increasing dietary salt would &lt;strong&gt;worsen edema and hypertension&lt;/strong&gt;; therefore this is inappropriate.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Monitor only for low potassium&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Corticosteroids can lead to &lt;strong&gt;hypokalemia&lt;/strong&gt;, but it is not the &lt;strong&gt;first or only reaction to monitor&lt;/strong&gt; immediately after administration. Glucose monitoring takes priority because hyperglycemia can occur rapidly and may require urgent adjustment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After IV hydrocortisone, the priority action is to &lt;strong&gt;check the patient&amp;rsquo;s blood glucose levels&lt;/strong&gt;, because corticosteroids can quickly raise glucose and trigger hyperglycemia, especially in vulnerable patients.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 30&amp;ndash;70 years&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Under the &lt;strong&gt;NP-NCD (National Programme for Prevention &amp;amp; Control of Non-Communicable Diseases)&lt;/strong&gt;, the Government of India conducts population-based screening for major NCDs such as &lt;strong&gt;hypertension, diabetes, oral cancer, breast cancer, and cervical cancer&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The programme specifically targets the age group &lt;strong&gt;30&amp;ndash;70 years&lt;/strong&gt; because the risk of developing lifestyle-related chronic diseases increases significantly in this phase of life.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This screening helps in &lt;strong&gt;early detection, timely referral, and long-term management&lt;/strong&gt; of NCDs to reduce morbidity and premature mortality.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Community health workers (ASHA / ANM) and Health &amp;amp; Wellness Centers play a major role in providing &lt;strong&gt;annual screening to individuals aged 30&amp;ndash;70 years&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;18&amp;ndash;30 years&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This group is not the primary target population of NP-NCD, as the risk of NCDs is comparatively lower.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;45&amp;ndash;80 years&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Although many NCDs occur in this group, the official government screening age range is &lt;strong&gt;30&amp;ndash;70 years&lt;/strong&gt;, not 45&amp;ndash;80.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;15&amp;ndash;60 years&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This wide range is &lt;strong&gt;not aligned with the NP-NCD screening guidelines&lt;/strong&gt; and includes age groups not routinely screened under the programme.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The NP-NCD programme of India focuses on screening individuals in the &lt;strong&gt;30&amp;ndash;70 year age group&lt;/strong&gt; to detect and manage non-communicable diseases early and reduce long-term complications and premature deaths.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Turner syndrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A karyotype of &lt;strong&gt;45,XO&lt;/strong&gt; is the classic chromosomal pattern seen in &lt;strong&gt;Turner syndrome&lt;/strong&gt;, which results from the complete or partial absence of one X chromosome.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Women with Turner syndrome commonly present with &lt;strong&gt;ovarian failure, infertility, and amenorrhea&lt;/strong&gt; (often primary, but can sometimes be secondary).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The absence of normal ovarian follicular function results in &lt;strong&gt;low estrogen and high gonadotropins (FSH/LH)&lt;/strong&gt;, causing menstrual irregularities and eventual amenorrhea.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Turner syndrome features may also include &lt;strong&gt;short stature, streak ovaries, delayed puberty, webbed neck, and cardiovascular anomalies&lt;/strong&gt;, although the degree of physical traits varies among individuals.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Asherman syndrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Asherman syndrome is caused by &lt;strong&gt;intrauterine adhesions&lt;/strong&gt; after uterine surgery or infection. The karyotype is &lt;strong&gt;normal (46,XX)&lt;/strong&gt;, not 45,XO.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Klinefelter syndrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Klinefelter syndrome occurs in &lt;strong&gt;males&lt;/strong&gt; and has the karyotype &lt;strong&gt;47,XXY&lt;/strong&gt;, not 45,XO.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Androgen insensitivity syndrome&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This condition occurs in individuals who are genetically male (&lt;strong&gt;46,XY&lt;/strong&gt;) but phenotypically female due to androgen receptor defects, not 45,XO.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A 45,XO karyotype conclusively indicates &lt;strong&gt;Turner syndrome&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the most likely diagnosis in a patient presenting with amenorrhea.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: A patient with trouble breathing and very small (pinpoint) pupils&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In mass-casualty incidents, the &lt;strong&gt;START (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment)&lt;/strong&gt; system is used to rapidly categorize victims based on the urgency of care needed. This system prioritizes victims by assessing &lt;strong&gt;Respiration&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;Perfusion&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;Mental status&lt;/strong&gt; (RPM).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A patient who has &lt;strong&gt;trouble breathing&lt;/strong&gt; is immediately placed in the &lt;strong&gt;Red (Immediate)&lt;/strong&gt; category. Problems with breathing indicate a &lt;strong&gt;life-threatening compromise&lt;/strong&gt; requiring urgent intervention to maintain airway and oxygenation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The presence of &lt;strong&gt;pinpoint pupils&lt;/strong&gt; along with respiratory difficulty may indicate possible &lt;strong&gt;opioid toxicity, head injury, or neurological impairment&lt;/strong&gt;, all of which can rapidly become fatal without immediate management.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;START triage principles emphasize that any victim with &lt;strong&gt;respiratory distress, inadequate breathing effort, or airway obstruction&lt;/strong&gt; must be tagged Red, regardless of other injuries, because airway compromise is the fastest cause of preventable death.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, a patient with &lt;strong&gt;respiratory difficulty&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;neurological signs&lt;/strong&gt; such as pinpoint pupils requires &lt;strong&gt;immediate life-saving interventions&lt;/strong&gt;, such as airway stabilization, assisted ventilation, or antidote administration, making this individual the priority case.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A patient who is walking with a fractured arm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: START triage dictates that any victim who can &lt;strong&gt;walk&lt;/strong&gt; is automatically categorized as &lt;strong&gt;Green (Minor)&lt;/strong&gt;. Although the fractured arm requires care, it is not immediately life-threatening.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A burn victim with 80% TBSA burns but stable vital signs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: In mass-casualty settings, burns greater than &lt;strong&gt;60&amp;ndash;70% TBSA&lt;/strong&gt; are considered &lt;strong&gt;non-survivable&lt;/strong&gt; due to poor prognosis. While the patient needs care, START triage places such victims under &lt;strong&gt;Expectant (Black/Grey)&lt;/strong&gt;, not immediate priority.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A patient in cardiac arrest with no pulse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: In START triage, &lt;strong&gt;CPR is not performed&lt;/strong&gt; during a mass-casualty incident. A patient with &lt;strong&gt;no pulse&lt;/strong&gt; or &lt;strong&gt;not breathing after repositioning the airway&lt;/strong&gt; is tagged as &lt;strong&gt;Black (Deceased/Expectant)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Under the START triage system, victims with &lt;strong&gt;respiratory compromise&lt;/strong&gt; or airway obstruction are always given &lt;strong&gt;Immediate (Red)&lt;/strong&gt; status because ensuring adequate breathing is the highest priority for survival. The patient with trouble breathing and pinpoint pupils requires the fastest life-saving intervention and therefore receives the highest priority in emergency triage.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Paralysis or weakness with loss of sensation in both legs&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A spinal cord injury at the &lt;strong&gt;T4 (thoracic 4)&lt;/strong&gt; level typically results in loss of motor and sensory function &lt;strong&gt;below the level of injury&lt;/strong&gt;. Because the thoracic spinal cord controls &lt;strong&gt;trunk and lower limb&lt;/strong&gt; function, an injury here most commonly leads to &lt;strong&gt;paraplegia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In paraplegia, the &lt;strong&gt;lower extremities&lt;/strong&gt; lose strength, movement, and sensation, while the &lt;strong&gt;arms and upper body remain intact&lt;/strong&gt; since they are supplied by cervical nerves above the injury level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A T4 injury does &lt;strong&gt;not affect the phrenic nerve (C3&amp;ndash;C5)&lt;/strong&gt;, which controls the diaphragm. Therefore, breathing remains mostly preserved, although &lt;strong&gt;intercostal muscle weakness&lt;/strong&gt; may reduce respiratory efficiency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because thoracic injuries do not involve nerve roots responsible for arm and hand functions (C5&amp;ndash;T1), &lt;strong&gt;upper limb strength, grip, and fine motor function remain normal&lt;/strong&gt;. This pattern clearly points toward lower-limb paralysis as the expected complication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, the defining feature of a complete spinal cord injury at the thoracic level is &lt;strong&gt;loss of movement and sensation in both legs&lt;/strong&gt;, making paraplegia the most expected outcome.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Weak hand grip only&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hand grip strength is controlled by &lt;strong&gt;cervical nerves (C6&amp;ndash;C8)&lt;/strong&gt;. A T4 injury occurs below the cervical region and does &lt;strong&gt;not affect upper limb function&lt;/strong&gt;, so this finding is not expected.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Severe weakness in both arms&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Arm and shoulder movements are governed by the &lt;strong&gt;brachial plexus (C5&amp;ndash;T1)&lt;/strong&gt;. Since T4 is below this area, a thoracic injury does not impair arm strength. Therefore, upper-limb weakness is inconsistent with a T4 injury.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Paralysis of the diaphragm needing a ventilator&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The diaphragm is controlled by the &lt;strong&gt;phrenic nerve (C3, C4, C5)&lt;/strong&gt;. Only high cervical injuries threaten diaphragm function or require ventilatory support. A T4 injury is far below the phrenic nerve level and &lt;strong&gt;does not cause diaphragmatic paralysis&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A complete spinal cord injury at the &lt;strong&gt;T4 level&lt;/strong&gt; results in &lt;strong&gt;paraplegia&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;marked by loss of motor function and sensation in the lower limbs&amp;mdash;while upper limb and diaphragmatic function remain preserved. Therefore, the expected clinical finding is paralysis or loss of sensation in both legs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: The patient is showing signs of diabetes insipidus&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Diabetes insipidus (DI) is characterized by the inability of the kidneys to concentrate urine due to either &lt;strong&gt;low ADH secretion&lt;/strong&gt; (central DI) or &lt;strong&gt;renal resistance to ADH&lt;/strong&gt; (nephrogenic DI). As a result, patients excrete large volumes of &lt;strong&gt;dilute urine&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to progressive &lt;strong&gt;water loss&lt;/strong&gt; and rising &lt;strong&gt;serum sodium levels&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A serum sodium of &lt;strong&gt;155 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt; indicates significant &lt;strong&gt;hypernatremia&lt;/strong&gt;, commonly seen when the body loses free water faster than sodium, a hallmark of &lt;strong&gt;untreated or evolving DI&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Although the patient has been NPO, the degree of hypernatremia is &lt;strong&gt;too high to be explained by simple fasting&lt;/strong&gt;. Fasting for 8 hours does not normally raise sodium to 155 mEq/L unless the patient is already losing water abnormally.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Serum osmolarity of &lt;strong&gt;300 mOsm/L&lt;/strong&gt; is on the higher side of normal and supports a state of &lt;strong&gt;hyperosmolar dehydration&lt;/strong&gt;, which occurs early in DI before severe osmolarity changes develop.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A normal urine specific gravity in the presence of hypernatremia suggests that the kidneys are &lt;strong&gt;failing to produce adequately concentrated urine&lt;/strong&gt; despite the body&amp;#39;s strong physiological need to conserve water. This mismatch is a key early indicator of &lt;strong&gt;developing diabetes insipidus&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thirst is normal because the patient is NPO&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: NPO status causes mild thirst, but it does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; cause serum sodium to rise as high as &lt;strong&gt;155 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt;. Normal physiological dehydration cannot explain such a high sodium level; therefore, NPO alone is not an adequate explanation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The kidneys are failing to work properly&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Kidney failure typically results in &lt;strong&gt;high urine specific gravity&lt;/strong&gt; due to retained solutes or &lt;strong&gt;very low urine output&lt;/strong&gt;. In this case, the kidneys appear to be excreting dilute urine relative to the patient&amp;rsquo;s hydration needs, which is more consistent with DI than renal failure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;ADH levels are elevated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Elevated ADH leads to &lt;strong&gt;water retention&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;low serum sodium&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;high urine specific gravity&lt;/strong&gt;. The patient&amp;#39;s hypernatremia and inability to concentrate urine suggest &lt;strong&gt;low or ineffective ADH&lt;/strong&gt;, the opposite of elevated ADH.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The combination of &lt;strong&gt;marked hypernatremia&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;inadequate urine concentration&lt;/strong&gt;, and intense thirst strongly suggests a developing picture of &lt;strong&gt;diabetes insipidus&lt;/strong&gt;. This condition reflects a failure of ADH action, causing excessive free-water loss and rising serum sodium levels despite normal or increased urine output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Potassium (K+)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Metabolic acidosis causes the body to move &lt;strong&gt;hydrogen ions (H+)&lt;/strong&gt; into the cells in an attempt to buffer excess acid in the bloodstream. When H+ shifts intracellularly, &lt;strong&gt;potassium (K+)&lt;/strong&gt; moves out of the cells to maintain electrical balance, leading to &lt;strong&gt;hyperkalemia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Even though the patient&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;pH is normal&lt;/strong&gt;, this indicates a state of &lt;strong&gt;compensated metabolic acidosis&lt;/strong&gt;, meaning the kidneys and lungs have balanced the pH, but the underlying disturbance&amp;mdash;acidosis&amp;mdash;still influences electrolyte movement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Patients with both &lt;strong&gt;hypertension&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;diabetes&lt;/strong&gt; are at increased risk for &lt;strong&gt;renal impairment&lt;/strong&gt;, which further reduces their ability to excrete excess potassium. This makes &lt;strong&gt;potassium monitoring critical&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent life-threatening arrhythmias.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Abnormal potassium levels, especially hyperkalemia, can cause &lt;strong&gt;dangerous ECG changes&lt;/strong&gt; such as peaked T waves, widened QRS complexes, and ventricular arrhythmias, making rapid monitoring and correction essential.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, in compensated metabolic acidosis&amp;mdash;especially in a diabetic and hypertensive patient&amp;mdash;the electrolyte that requires the closest and most urgent monitoring is &lt;strong&gt;serum potassium&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Calcium (Ca2+)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Acidosis may increase ionized calcium slightly, but these changes are usually mild and not the primary concern. Calcium does not pose an immediate danger compared to &lt;strong&gt;rapid potassium shifts&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sodium (Na+)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Sodium levels are generally not significantly altered by metabolic acidosis. While renal disease and diabetes can affect sodium balance, it does not require &lt;strong&gt;urgent&lt;/strong&gt; monitoring the way potassium does.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Magnesium (Mg2+)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Magnesium can influence muscle and cardiac function, but acidosis does not cause rapid, dangerous shifts in magnesium levels. Monitoring may be useful but is &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; the priority in acute compensated acidosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because metabolic acidosis causes &lt;strong&gt;K+ to shift out of cells&lt;/strong&gt; and diabetes/hypertension increase the risk of &lt;strong&gt;renal dysfunction&lt;/strong&gt;, the electrolyte most likely to reach dangerous levels is &lt;strong&gt;potassium&lt;/strong&gt;. Continuous monitoring is essential to prevent fatal cardiac complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Check the patient&amp;rsquo;s lithium level right away&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Lithium has a &lt;strong&gt;narrow therapeutic index&lt;/strong&gt;, meaning even small increases above the therapeutic range can quickly lead to &lt;strong&gt;toxicity&lt;/strong&gt;. Symptoms such as &lt;strong&gt;nausea, drowsiness, muscle weakness, and a coarse tremor&lt;/strong&gt; are classic early indicators of &lt;strong&gt;lithium toxicity&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because lithium toxicity can rapidly progress to &lt;strong&gt;severe neurologic impairment&lt;/strong&gt; including confusion, seizures, coma, and cardiac collapse, the nurse must immediately verify the &lt;strong&gt;serum lithium level&lt;/strong&gt; to assess the severity and determine the need for urgent intervention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Obtaining a lithium level is essential before making any changes to dosing or fluid intake because lithium concentrations directly guide whether treatment requires &lt;strong&gt;dose hold, hydration therapy, or emergency management&lt;/strong&gt; such as hemodialysis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Early detection and confirmation through &lt;strong&gt;serum lithium monitoring&lt;/strong&gt; are critical to prevent complications, making it the top nursing priority when toxicity is suspected.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the first and most important action is to &lt;strong&gt;check the lithium level immediately&lt;/strong&gt; to confirm toxicity and guide subsequent medical management.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Give the next lithium dose and increase oral fluids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Administering another dose can &lt;strong&gt;worsen toxicity&lt;/strong&gt; and is unsafe when symptoms of lithium excess are already present. Increasing fluids may help later, but not before confirming the serum level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Provide an anti-anxiety medicine to reduce the tremor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A coarse tremor is a warning sign of &lt;strong&gt;lithium toxicity&lt;/strong&gt;, not anxiety. Giving anxiolytics would treat the symptom instead of the cause and may mask clinical deterioration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Reduce the patient&amp;rsquo;s sodium intake and monitor fluids&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Lowering sodium intake causes the kidneys to &lt;strong&gt;retain lithium&lt;/strong&gt;, which can further elevate levels and increase toxicity. This would worsen the patient&amp;rsquo;s condition and is contraindicated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Signs such as &lt;strong&gt;coarse tremor, nausea, drowsiness, and weakness&lt;/strong&gt; strongly indicate early lithium toxicity. Because lithium levels guide all subsequent treatment decisions, the priority nursing action is to &lt;strong&gt;check the serum lithium level immediately&lt;/strong&gt; to confirm toxicity and initiate safe, timely care.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Give the calcium slowly over 10&amp;ndash;15 minutes while closely watching the heart rhythm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A potassium level of &lt;strong&gt;6.3 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt; indicates &lt;strong&gt;moderate to severe hyperkalemia&lt;/strong&gt;, a condition that can quickly lead to &lt;strong&gt;fatal cardiac arrhythmias&lt;/strong&gt; such as ventricular tachycardia, ventricular fibrillation, or cardiac arrest.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Intravenous &lt;strong&gt;calcium gluconate or calcium chloride&lt;/strong&gt; does not reduce potassium levels, but it provides immediate protection by &lt;strong&gt;stabilizing the cardiac membrane&lt;/strong&gt; against the effects of elevated potassium.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To prevent complications such as bradycardia, heart block, or cardiac arrest, IV calcium must be given &lt;strong&gt;slowly over 10&amp;ndash;15 minutes&lt;/strong&gt; while continuously monitoring the patient&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;ECG&lt;/strong&gt; for any signs of improvement or worsening cardiac irritability.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rapid IV push of calcium is dangerous because it can cause &lt;strong&gt;severe hypotension, arrhythmias, or cardiac standstill&lt;/strong&gt;. Slow administration ensures safe cardiac stabilization during hyperkalemic crises.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the nurse&amp;rsquo;s immediate priority is to &lt;strong&gt;administer IV calcium slowly with continuous cardiac monitoring&lt;/strong&gt; to protect the heart while other hyperkalemia-correcting treatments are prepared.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Give the calcium as a fast IV push&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A rapid IV push can cause &lt;strong&gt;dangerous bradyarrhythmias&lt;/strong&gt; or cardiac standstill. It is unsafe and contraindicated because calcium must be infused &lt;strong&gt;slowly&lt;/strong&gt; in hyperkalemia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Delay the calcium and recheck potassium in an hour&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hyperkalemia above &lt;strong&gt;6.0 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt; is a true medical emergency. Delaying calcium increases the risk of &lt;strong&gt;sudden cardiac arrest&lt;/strong&gt;. Immediate treatment is required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Provide oral calcium along with a potassium-binding drug&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Oral calcium works too slowly to stabilize the heart and is not appropriate in emergencies. Potassium binders help reduce potassium but &lt;strong&gt;do not provide rapid cardiac protection&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hyperkalemia with a potassium level of &lt;strong&gt;6.3 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt; poses immediate cardiac danger. The priority is to &lt;strong&gt;stabilize the myocardium&lt;/strong&gt; by administering IV calcium &lt;strong&gt;slowly over 10&amp;ndash;15 minutes while continuously monitoring the ECG&lt;/strong&gt;, ensuring rapid and safe cardiac protection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Erythromycin 0.5% eye ointment&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Chlamydia trachomatis is a common sexually transmitted infection that can be passed from mother to infant during vaginal delivery, leading to &lt;strong&gt;ophthalmia neonatorum&lt;/strong&gt;, a serious newborn eye infection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To prevent this infection, newborns receive routine &lt;strong&gt;prophylactic antibiotic eye ointment&lt;/strong&gt; immediately after birth. The most widely recommended and effective medication is &lt;strong&gt;erythromycin 0.5% ophthalmic ointment&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Erythromycin eye ointment works by preventing growth of &lt;strong&gt;Chlamydia trachomatis&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Neisseria gonorrhoeae&lt;/strong&gt; in the conjunctiva, reducing the risk of conjunctivitis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because the baby is currently &lt;strong&gt;asymptomatic&lt;/strong&gt;, only topical prophylaxis is required at birth. Systemic antibiotics are reserved for infants who develop symptoms such as pneumonia or conjunctivitis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, applying &lt;strong&gt;erythromycin 0.5% eye ointment&lt;/strong&gt; immediately after delivery is the correct and recommended measure to prevent ophthalmia neonatorum in newborns of mothers with chlamydial infection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Tetracycline drops&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Tetracycline eye drops were used in the past but are &lt;strong&gt;no longer recommended&lt;/strong&gt; in many countries due to lower effectiveness and limited availability.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Oral erythromycin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Oral erythromycin is used when the newborn shows &lt;strong&gt;clinical symptoms&lt;/strong&gt; of chlamydial infection (such as conjunctivitis or pneumonia). It is &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; used for routine asymptomatic prophylaxis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Silver nitrate 1% solution&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Silver nitrate prevents &lt;strong&gt;gonococcal&lt;/strong&gt; infection but is &lt;strong&gt;not effective&lt;/strong&gt; against chlamydia and may cause chemical conjunctivitis. It is no longer recommended in modern practice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;For a newborn delivered by a mother who is positive for &lt;strong&gt;Chlamydia trachomatis&lt;/strong&gt;, the safest and most effective prophylactic treatment is &lt;strong&gt;erythromycin 0.5% eye ointment&lt;/strong&gt;, which helps prevent ophthalmia neonatorum while avoiding unnecessary systemic medication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Carrier&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A person who is infected with a &lt;strong&gt;pathogenic organism&lt;/strong&gt; but does not show any &lt;strong&gt;clinical signs or symptoms&lt;/strong&gt; is referred to as a &lt;strong&gt;carrier&lt;/strong&gt;. Despite the absence of illness, this individual can still &lt;strong&gt;transmit the infection&lt;/strong&gt; to others, making carriers an important factor in the spread of communicable diseases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Carriers play a major role in public health because they can unknowingly contribute to the &lt;strong&gt;silent transmission&lt;/strong&gt; of infections such as hepatitis B, typhoid, HIV, meningococcal disease, and COVID-19.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In epidemiology, carriers may be &lt;strong&gt;temporary&lt;/strong&gt; (short-term infection) or &lt;strong&gt;chronic&lt;/strong&gt; (long-term infection). Chronic carriers can harbor organisms for extended periods, making them a significant source of continuous spread in the community.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because carriers do not show symptoms, they are often not diagnosed or isolated, increasing the risk of infecting &lt;strong&gt;susceptible individuals&lt;/strong&gt; who may develop severe disease.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, a person who is infected, asymptomatic, yet capable of transmitting disease is correctly classified as a &lt;strong&gt;carrier&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Vector&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A vector is an &lt;strong&gt;animal or insect&lt;/strong&gt;, such as a mosquito or tick, that transmits infectious agents from one host to another. Vectors do not describe infected humans.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Host&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A host is any human or organism in which a pathogen lives and multiplies. A host may or may not show symptoms, but the term does &lt;strong&gt;not specifically imply asymptomatic transmission&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Case&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A case refers to a person who is &lt;strong&gt;clinically ill and symptomatic&lt;/strong&gt; due to an infectious disease. This is the opposite of a carrier, who shows no symptoms.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A person who harbors a disease-causing organism, remains asymptomatic, yet can spread infection to others is known as a &lt;strong&gt;carrier&lt;/strong&gt;. Carriers are key contributors to hidden transmission chains and are an important focus of public health surveillance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Antineoplastic medications&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Antineoplastic medications are drugs specifically designed to &lt;strong&gt;slow down, inhibit, or stop the growth of cancer cells&lt;/strong&gt;. They work by targeting rapidly dividing cells and are widely used in &lt;strong&gt;chemotherapy&lt;/strong&gt; for the treatment of various malignancies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;These medications interfere with essential processes of cancer cells such as &lt;strong&gt;DNA replication, RNA synthesis, and mitosis&lt;/strong&gt;, thereby preventing further cell division and tumor growth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because cancer cells divide more rapidly than most normal cells, antineoplastic agents selectively target these high-rate division processes, making them &lt;strong&gt;effective in controlling cancer progression&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Common classes of antineoplastic drugs include &lt;strong&gt;alkylating agents, antimetabolites, mitotic inhibitors, topoisomerase inhibitors, and antitumor antibiotics&lt;/strong&gt;, each working at different points of the cancer cell cycle.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, medications that primarily act by &lt;strong&gt;blocking cell division&lt;/strong&gt; and inhibiting cancer growth are appropriately termed &lt;strong&gt;antineoplastic medications&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Antipyretic medications&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Antipyretics are used to &lt;strong&gt;reduce fever&lt;/strong&gt; by acting on the hypothalamus. They do not affect cancer cell growth or cell division.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Antiemetic medications&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Antiemetics help &lt;strong&gt;prevent nausea and vomiting&lt;/strong&gt;, commonly used during chemotherapy. They do not kill or suppress cancer cells.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Antihistaminic medications&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Antihistamines block the effects of &lt;strong&gt;histamine&lt;/strong&gt; and are used for allergies. They have no role in controlling cancer or inhibiting cell division.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Drugs that work by &lt;strong&gt;inhibiting cell division and preventing cancer growth&lt;/strong&gt; are known as &lt;strong&gt;antineoplastic medications&lt;/strong&gt;. These agents form the foundation of chemotherapy and are essential in the treatment and control of malignant diseases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: First-degree AV block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A first-degree AV block is characterized by a &lt;strong&gt;consistently prolonged PR interval&lt;/strong&gt; greater than &lt;strong&gt;0.20 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;, with every &lt;strong&gt;P wave followed by a QRS complex&lt;/strong&gt;. This means that electrical conduction through the AV node is delayed but not interrupted.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this case, the PR interval is &lt;strong&gt;0.24 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;, which confirms a significant conduction delay but still maintains a one-to-one atrioventricular conduction pattern.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This rhythm is generally benign and is often caused by conditions such as &lt;strong&gt;increased vagal tone, beta-blockers, calcium-channel blockers, ischemia, or aging of the conduction system&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because the PR interval remains &lt;strong&gt;constant and prolonged&lt;/strong&gt; on every beat without any dropped QRS complexes, it fits the classic presentation of a first-degree AV block.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, a stable PR interval that is longer than normal but associated with a normal QRS response confirms the diagnosis of &lt;strong&gt;first-degree AV block&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Atrial fibrillation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Atrial fibrillation has &lt;strong&gt;no identifiable P waves&lt;/strong&gt; and produces an &lt;strong&gt;irregularly irregular rhythm&lt;/strong&gt;. It does not have a measurable PR interval, so it cannot match this ECG description.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Second-degree AV block (Mobitz I)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Mobitz I (Wenckebach) shows a PR interval that becomes &lt;strong&gt;progressively longer&lt;/strong&gt; until a &lt;strong&gt;QRS is dropped&lt;/strong&gt;. In this scenario, the PR interval is &lt;strong&gt;constant&lt;/strong&gt; and no QRS complexes are missing, ruling out Mobitz I.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Complete (third-degree) AV block&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Third-degree AV block features &lt;strong&gt;no relationship&lt;/strong&gt; between P waves and QRS complexes, with atria and ventricles beating independently. This does not match the described pattern of normal one-to-one conduction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A PR interval that is &lt;strong&gt;uniformly and consistently prolonged&lt;/strong&gt; on every beat, with each P wave followed by a QRS complex, is the hallmark of a &lt;strong&gt;first-degree AV block&lt;/strong&gt;. This identifies delayed AV conduction without interruption of ventricular activation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 24&amp;ndash;48 hours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After removal of a &lt;strong&gt;non-tunneled central venous catheter (CVC)&lt;/strong&gt;, the insertion tract remains open and functions like a small tunnel from the skin to a central vein. This creates a brief risk of &lt;strong&gt;air embolism&lt;/strong&gt; if air enters the venous system during inspiration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To prevent this complication, the site must be covered with an &lt;strong&gt;air-occlusive (air-tight) dressing&lt;/strong&gt; to ensure that negative intrathoracic pressure during breathing does not pull air into the venous circulation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The dressing should remain in place for &lt;strong&gt;24&amp;ndash;48 hours&lt;/strong&gt;, which is the time required for the &lt;strong&gt;subcutaneous tract to seal and for a stable clot&lt;/strong&gt; to form at the insertion site.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Maintaining an occlusive dressing for this duration also reduces the risk of &lt;strong&gt;bleeding, infection, and delayed air entry&lt;/strong&gt;, especially in patients on anticoagulants or with elevated venous pressure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, keeping the air-tight dressing in place for &lt;strong&gt;24&amp;ndash;48 hours&lt;/strong&gt; ensures both clot formation and complete closure of the insertion tract, preventing potentially fatal air embolism.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;6&amp;ndash;8 hours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This duration is &lt;strong&gt;too short&lt;/strong&gt; for the CVC tract to fully close. Air may still enter the venous system, especially when the patient sits up or takes deep breaths.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;1&amp;ndash;2 hours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is inadequate for clot stabilization or tract sealing. Removing the dressing after only a couple of hours significantly increases the risk of &lt;strong&gt;air embolism&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;72 hours (3 days)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: While safe, this time frame is &lt;strong&gt;longer than necessary&lt;/strong&gt;. The tract usually seals sufficiently by 24&amp;ndash;48 hours, and prolonged occlusion may increase moisture accumulation and infection risk.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;An &lt;strong&gt;air-tight occlusive dressing&lt;/strong&gt; must remain in place for &lt;strong&gt;24&amp;ndash;48 hours&lt;/strong&gt; after the removal of a non-tunneled CVC to prevent air embolism and allow complete clot formation and sealing of the catheter tract.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Keep the bulb squeezed (compressed) after emptying and then close the cap&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A Jackson-Pratt (J-P) drain works on the principle of &lt;strong&gt;closed-suction drainage&lt;/strong&gt;, meaning it must maintain &lt;strong&gt;continuous negative pressure&lt;/strong&gt; to remove fluid effectively from the surgical site.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After the drain is emptied, the nurse must &lt;strong&gt;squeeze (compress) the bulb&lt;/strong&gt; before closing the cap. This creates the &lt;strong&gt;vacuum suction&lt;/strong&gt; needed to pull fluid from the wound and into the bulb reservoir.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;If the bulb is not compressed after emptying, no suction is generated, and the drain becomes ineffective, increasing the risk of &lt;strong&gt;fluid accumulation, hematoma, seroma, and infection&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Proper functioning of a J-P drain ensures reduced postoperative complications by maintaining continuous, gentle suction throughout recovery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the essential nursing action is to &lt;strong&gt;compress the bulb and then close the cap&lt;/strong&gt; to re-establish suction.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Flush the tubing with sterile saline every shift&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: J-P drains are &lt;strong&gt;not designed to be flushed&lt;/strong&gt;. Flushing may introduce bacteria or disrupt the closed-suction system, increasing infection risk.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Leave the bulb fully expanded all the time&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A fully expanded bulb creates &lt;strong&gt;no suction&lt;/strong&gt;. This renders the drain nonfunctional and leads to fluid retention inside the wound.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Empty the drain only when it becomes completely full&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A J-P drain should be emptied when it is &lt;strong&gt;&amp;frac12; to &amp;frac34; full&lt;/strong&gt;. Allowing it to completely fill eliminates vacuum pressure and increases the risk of leakage and infection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To ensure proper suction and effective postoperative drainage, the nurse must &lt;strong&gt;compress the J-P drain bulb after emptying and then close the cap&lt;/strong&gt;. This restores negative pressure and maintains the function of the closed-suction system.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 6,400 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Parkland formula is the most commonly used method to calculate initial fluid resuscitation in adults with major burns. It states: &lt;strong&gt;4 mL &amp;times; body weight (kg) &amp;times; %TBSA (Total Body Surface Area) burned&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;For an adult weighing &lt;strong&gt;80 kg&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;40% TBSA&lt;/strong&gt; full-thickness burns, the total fluid requirement in the first 24 hours is calculated as:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;4 mL &amp;times; 80 &amp;times; 40 = 12,800 mL&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;According to the Parkland protocol, &lt;strong&gt;half of the total fluid&lt;/strong&gt; must be administered in the &lt;strong&gt;first 8 hours&lt;/strong&gt; from the time of injury, not from hospital arrival.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Half of 12,800 mL is:&lt;br /&gt;
	&lt;strong&gt;12,800 &amp;divide; 2 = 6,400 mL&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This early aggressive fluid resuscitation is essential to maintain &lt;strong&gt;tissue perfusion, prevent burn shock&lt;/strong&gt;, and support adequate urine output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;12,800 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is the &lt;strong&gt;total 24-hour fluid requirement&lt;/strong&gt; using the Parkland formula, not the volume required in the first 8 hours.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;3,200 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This represents &lt;strong&gt;one-fourth&lt;/strong&gt; of the total fluid requirement. It does not match any phase of the Parkland distribution schedule.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;1,600 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This volume is too low and would result in &lt;strong&gt;severe under-resuscitation&lt;/strong&gt;, risking burn shock and organ failure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Using the Parkland formula, an 80-kg adult with 40% TBSA burns requires &lt;strong&gt;6,400 mL of Lactated Ringer&amp;rsquo;s solution in the first 8 hours&lt;/strong&gt; to ensure adequate fluid resuscitation and prevent burn shock.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Add 1 mL of the 1:1,000 solution to 9 mL of normal saline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A 1:1,000 adrenaline solution contains &lt;strong&gt;1 mg of epinephrine per 1 mL&lt;/strong&gt;. To prepare a 1:10,000 solution, the concentration must be diluted to &lt;strong&gt;0.1 mg/mL&lt;/strong&gt;, which is ten times more dilute than the original.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To achieve this, the original solution must be diluted in a &lt;strong&gt;1:10 ratio&lt;/strong&gt;. This means 1 part of the drug must be mixed with 9 parts of diluent to make a total of 10 parts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Adding &lt;strong&gt;1 mL&lt;/strong&gt; of the 1:1,000 adrenaline to &lt;strong&gt;9 mL of normal saline&lt;/strong&gt; creates a total volume of 10 mL, containing 1 mg diluted tenfold, giving a final strength of &lt;strong&gt;1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This is the standard and safest method used in emergency settings for preparing IV adrenaline for &lt;strong&gt;cardiac arrest, anaphylaxis, and resuscitation&lt;/strong&gt; protocols.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, diluting &lt;strong&gt;1 mL of 1:1,000 adrenaline with 9 mL of normal saline&lt;/strong&gt; provides the correct 1:10,000 concentration required for IV administration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Add 0.5 mL of the 1:1,000 solution to 5 mL of normal saline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This creates a total volume of 5.5 mL, resulting in a concentration stronger than 1:10,000. It does not achieve the required dilution ratio.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Mix 0.1 mL of the 1:1,000 solution with 10 mL of normal saline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This produces an extremely weak solution (&lt;strong&gt;0.01 mg in 10 mL&lt;/strong&gt;), far more dilute than 1:10,000. It is not clinically appropriate for IV adrenaline preparation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Add 1 mL of the 1:1,000 solution to 99 mL of normal saline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This creates a 1:100,000 solution, which is ten times weaker than needed. It is commonly used for local anesthesia with adrenaline, not emergency IV dosing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To prepare a proper &lt;strong&gt;1:10,000 adrenaline solution&lt;/strong&gt; from a 1:1,000 stock, the correct method is mixing &lt;strong&gt;1 mL of the original solution with 9 mL of normal saline&lt;/strong&gt;, ensuring safe and accurate dosing during emergency care.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Printer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In computer hardware, an &lt;strong&gt;output device&lt;/strong&gt; is any device that receives processed data from the computer and presents it in a human-readable form such as text, graphics, or physical copies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;printer&lt;/strong&gt; is the most common output device because it takes digital information from the computer and produces a &lt;strong&gt;hard copy&lt;/strong&gt; (printed document or image) on paper.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Output devices do not send data into the computer; instead, they &lt;strong&gt;display, project, or print&lt;/strong&gt; information created by the system.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Printers come in several types&amp;mdash;such as &lt;strong&gt;inkjet, laser, dot matrix&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;but all perform the same essential function of converting electronic data into physical output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, among the given options, the device that clearly functions as an &lt;strong&gt;output unit&lt;/strong&gt; is the &lt;strong&gt;printer&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Mouse&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A mouse is an &lt;strong&gt;input device&lt;/strong&gt; that sends data (movement and clicks) to the computer. It does not produce output.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Keyboard&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A keyboard is an &lt;strong&gt;input device&lt;/strong&gt; used to enter text and commands into the computer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Scanner&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A scanner converts physical documents into digital form, making it an &lt;strong&gt;input device&lt;/strong&gt;, not an output device.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Among the listed options, the only device that provides information &lt;strong&gt;from the computer to the user&lt;/strong&gt; is the &lt;strong&gt;printer&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct example of an output device.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Start Magnesium Sulfate infusion and get ready for delivery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A pregnant woman at &lt;strong&gt;36 weeks&lt;/strong&gt; with blood pressure of &lt;strong&gt;160/110 mmHg&lt;/strong&gt;, proteinuria, and a &lt;strong&gt;recent tonic-clonic seizure&lt;/strong&gt; meets the diagnostic criteria for &lt;strong&gt;eclampsia&lt;/strong&gt;, a life-threatening obstetric emergency requiring immediate intervention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Magnesium sulfate&lt;/strong&gt; is the drug of choice for preventing further seizures because it stabilizes the neuromuscular system and reduces CNS irritability. It is the first-line and most critical intervention in eclampsia management.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;At 36 weeks, the fetus is sufficiently mature, and eclampsia poses significant risk to both mother and baby. Therefore, once the mother is stabilized with magnesium sulfate, preparations for &lt;strong&gt;delivery&lt;/strong&gt; should begin promptly, as &lt;strong&gt;definitive treatment for eclampsia is delivery&lt;/strong&gt; of the fetus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Delaying delivery or withholding magnesium sulfate increases the risk of further seizures, maternal stroke, placental abruption, fetal distress, and death. Immediate seizure control and expedited delivery are the safest actions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, initiating &lt;strong&gt;Magnesium Sulfate infusion&lt;/strong&gt; and preparing for &lt;strong&gt;delivery&lt;/strong&gt; is the top priority in managing this patient with eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Give labetalol and observe her for the next 24 hours&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Antihypertensives like labetalol help control blood pressure but do &lt;strong&gt;not prevent seizures&lt;/strong&gt;. Observation alone is unsafe because she already had a seizure and requires urgent magnesium therapy and delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Perform an amniocentesis to assess fetal lung maturity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This delays lifesaving treatment. At &lt;strong&gt;36 weeks&lt;/strong&gt;, lungs are typically mature, and eclampsia is an emergency requiring &lt;strong&gt;immediate stabilization and delivery&lt;/strong&gt;, not further testing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Keep her on strict bed rest in a quiet room and monitor the fetal heart rate&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Although reducing stimulation is part of eclampsia care, it is &lt;strong&gt;not a priority&lt;/strong&gt; compared to controlling seizures with magnesium sulfate. Monitoring alone does not address the underlying emergency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A woman at 36 weeks with severe hypertension, proteinuria, and recent seizure is experiencing &lt;strong&gt;eclampsia&lt;/strong&gt;. The highest-priority intervention is to &lt;strong&gt;initiate Magnesium Sulfate therapy&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent further seizures and &lt;strong&gt;prepare for delivery&lt;/strong&gt;, which is the only definitive treatment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 20&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The given sequence is: &lt;strong&gt;2, 4, 8, 12, 16&lt;/strong&gt;. To identify the next number, we must analyze the pattern of increments between consecutive terms.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The differences between the numbers are:&lt;br /&gt;
	4 &amp;minus; 2 = &lt;strong&gt;+2&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
	8 &amp;minus; 4 = &lt;strong&gt;+4&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
	12 &amp;minus; 8 = &lt;strong&gt;+4&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;br /&gt;
	16 &amp;minus; 12 = &lt;strong&gt;+4&lt;/strong&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After the first increase of &lt;strong&gt;+2&lt;/strong&gt;, the sequence continues with a consistent increase of &lt;strong&gt;+4&lt;/strong&gt; for each subsequent term.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Following this established pattern, the next number should also increase by &lt;strong&gt;+4&lt;/strong&gt; from the last term.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, 16 + 4 = &lt;strong&gt;20&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the next number in the logical progression of the sequence.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam is widely known as the &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;Missile Man of India&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt; because of his exceptional contribution to India&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;missile and launch vehicle development programs&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;He played a key leadership role in major projects such as &lt;strong&gt;Agni and Prithvi missiles&lt;/strong&gt; under the Integrated Guided Missile Development Program (IGMDP), which significantly strengthened India&amp;rsquo;s defense capabilities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Apart from his scientific achievements, Dr. Kalam served as the &lt;strong&gt;11th President of India (2002&amp;ndash;2007)&lt;/strong&gt;, where he was admired for his humility, simplicity, and inspiring vision for national development.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;He also contributed to India&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;space research&lt;/strong&gt; through his work with ISRO on the &lt;strong&gt;SLV-3 launch vehicle&lt;/strong&gt;, which successfully placed the Rohini satellite into orbit.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Due to his outstanding contributions to science, defense, and public service, he remains one of India&amp;rsquo;s most respected and inspirational personalities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dr. Homi J. Bhabha&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Known as the &lt;strong&gt;Father of India&amp;rsquo;s Nuclear Program&lt;/strong&gt;, but not associated with missile development or presidency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dr. C.V. Raman&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A Nobel Prize&amp;ndash;winning physicist known for the &lt;strong&gt;Raman Effect&lt;/strong&gt;, not for missile technology or presidency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dr. Vikram Sarabhai&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Known as the &lt;strong&gt;Father of India&amp;rsquo;s Space Program&lt;/strong&gt;, but not given the title &amp;ldquo;Missile Man,&amp;rdquo; nor was he President of India.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Indian scientist honored as the &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;Missile Man of India&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt; for leading missile technology development and who also served as the &lt;strong&gt;11th President&lt;/strong&gt; is &lt;strong&gt;Dr. A.P.J. Abdul Kalam&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: McRoberts maneuver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;turtle sign&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; occurs when the baby&amp;rsquo;s head retracts back toward the perineum immediately after birth of the head, indicating &lt;strong&gt;shoulder dystocia&lt;/strong&gt;, an obstetric emergency where the anterior shoulder becomes trapped behind the maternal pubic bone.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;McRoberts maneuver&lt;/strong&gt; is the recommended &lt;strong&gt;first-line intervention&lt;/strong&gt; because it is simple, safe, and effective. It works by sharply flexing the mother&amp;#39;s thighs onto her abdomen, which &lt;strong&gt;widens the pelvic outlet&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;straightens the sacrum&lt;/strong&gt;, allowing the baby&amp;rsquo;s shoulder to slip beneath the pubic symphysis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This maneuver resolves more than &lt;strong&gt;40&amp;ndash;50% of shoulder dystocia cases&lt;/strong&gt; without the need for additional, more invasive procedures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Early and correct use of the McRoberts maneuver reduces maternal and fetal complications such as &lt;strong&gt;brachial plexus injury, hypoxia, or postpartum hemorrhage&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, upon recognizing the turtle sign, the &lt;strong&gt;McRoberts maneuver&lt;/strong&gt; must be initiated immediately to relieve shoulder impaction safely.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Woods&amp;rsquo; screw maneuver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is an internal rotational maneuver used &lt;strong&gt;only if McRoberts and suprapubic pressure fail&lt;/strong&gt;. It rotates the fetal shoulders but is not the first step.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Applying fundal pressure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Fundal pressure is &lt;strong&gt;contraindicated&lt;/strong&gt; in shoulder dystocia because it can worsen impaction and cause severe fetal injury.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Zavanelli maneuver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This involves pushing the fetal head back into the vagina for emergency cesarean section. It is a &lt;strong&gt;last-resort, high-risk intervention&lt;/strong&gt; when all other attempts fail.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The presence of a &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;turtle sign&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; signals shoulder dystocia. The safest and most effective initial action is the &lt;strong&gt;McRoberts maneuver&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the first step in managing this obstetric emergency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Cocaine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The patient presents with a combination of symptoms strongly associated with &lt;strong&gt;cocaine intoxication&lt;/strong&gt;, including &lt;strong&gt;tachycardia (fast heart rate), dilated pupils, severe paranoia, agitation, and tactile hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; such as &amp;ldquo;insects crawling on the skin,&amp;rdquo; known as &lt;strong&gt;formication&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cocaine is a potent central nervous system stimulant that increases levels of &lt;strong&gt;dopamine, norepinephrine, and serotonin&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to intense sympathetic activation and psychiatric disturbances.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A classic physical finding in chronic cocaine snorting is &lt;strong&gt;nasal septum irritation, ulceration, or perforation&lt;/strong&gt; due to the drug&amp;rsquo;s strong vasoconstrictive effects, which reduce blood supply to nasal tissues.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The patient&amp;rsquo;s history of &lt;strong&gt;snorting a white powder&lt;/strong&gt;, combined with septal perforation and acute psychotic symptoms, closely matches the typical presentation of cocaine use.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the drug most likely responsible for this cluster of cardiovascular, neurological, and behavioral symptoms is &lt;strong&gt;cocaine&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Marijuana&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Marijuana may cause mild euphoria, relaxation, or anxiety, but it does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; cause septal perforation, severe paranoia with formication, or powerful sympathetic stimulation like cocaine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Heroin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Heroin is a &lt;strong&gt;depressant&lt;/strong&gt; that causes pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression, and sedation&amp;mdash;not dilated pupils, paranoia, or stimulant symptoms.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;LSD&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: LSD can cause hallucinations and dilated pupils but does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; produce nasal septum damage or tactile hallucinations (&amp;ldquo;bugs crawling&amp;rdquo;), which are far more characteristic of stimulant drugs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The combination of &lt;strong&gt;snorting a white powder, nasal septum perforation, paranoia, tachycardia, dilated pupils, and tactile hallucinations&lt;/strong&gt; is classic for &lt;strong&gt;cocaine intoxication&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the most likely responsible drug.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Skip the next lithium dose and come to the clinic right away for a lithium level test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Lithium has a &lt;strong&gt;very narrow therapeutic range&lt;/strong&gt;, and even slight increases in serum levels can cause toxicity. Symptoms such as &lt;strong&gt;nausea, vomiting, diarrhea, coarse hand tremor, weakness, and confusion&lt;/strong&gt; are classic signs of &lt;strong&gt;lithium toxicity&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;coarse tremor&lt;/strong&gt; is a key indicator of toxicity and is more serious than the fine tremor seen in stable lithium therapy, making immediate evaluation necessary.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Continuing the lithium dose may worsen toxicity and lead to severe complications such as &lt;strong&gt;seizures, arrhythmias, kidney failure, or coma&lt;/strong&gt;. Therefore, the patient should be advised to &lt;strong&gt;hold (skip) the next dose&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Prompt testing of the &lt;strong&gt;serum lithium level&lt;/strong&gt; is the safest and most appropriate step to confirm toxicity and guide urgent medical treatment, including hydration or potential hospitalization.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the correct advice is to &lt;strong&gt;stop the next dose&lt;/strong&gt; and visit the clinic immediately for a lithium level check.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Increase water intake and lower salt intake&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Reducing salt intake actually &lt;strong&gt;increases lithium levels&lt;/strong&gt;, making toxicity worse. Hydration is helpful but does &lt;strong&gt;not replace the need for urgent evaluation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Take the next lithium dose with food and plenty of water&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Continuing lithium during toxicity can lead to &lt;strong&gt;life-threatening complications&lt;/strong&gt;. Taking it with food only reduces stomach upset, not toxicity.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;These symptoms are normal and will go away soon&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: These symptoms are &lt;strong&gt;not normal&lt;/strong&gt; but strongly suggest toxicity. Reassurance without action is unsafe and dangerous.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Symptoms such as &lt;strong&gt;nausea, vomiting, and a coarse tremor&lt;/strong&gt; indicate possible lithium toxicity. The safest and most appropriate action is to &lt;strong&gt;hold the next dose&lt;/strong&gt; and seek immediate evaluation for a &lt;strong&gt;lithium level test&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent serious complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Gardasil&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The image shows a pre-filled syringe manufactured by &lt;strong&gt;Merck&lt;/strong&gt;, containing a suspension used for the prevention of infections caused by &lt;strong&gt;Human Papillomavirus (HPV)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Gardasil is a &lt;strong&gt;quadrivalent or nonavalent HPV vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; that protects against multiple HPV types, often listed on the packaging as &lt;strong&gt;6, 11, 16, 18, 31, 33, 45, 52, and 58&lt;/strong&gt;. These types are responsible for genital warts and various cancers, particularly &lt;strong&gt;cervical cancer&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The syringe label clearly lists several HPV types and protein quantities, which is a characteristic feature of &lt;strong&gt;Gardasil (HPV vaccine)&lt;/strong&gt; packaging.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Gardasil is provided as a &lt;strong&gt;0.5 mL single-dose syringe&lt;/strong&gt; for intramuscular injection, matching the presentation shown in the image.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on the vaccine composition, manufacturer, and labeling, the product shown is identified as &lt;strong&gt;Gardasil&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Heparin&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Heparin is an anticoagulant used to prevent clot formation. It does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; come labeled with HPV types, nor is it packaged by Merck in this format.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Montax&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Montax is not related to HPV vaccination and does not match the syringe design, labeling, or description present in the image.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The syringe labeled with multiple &lt;strong&gt;HPV protein types&lt;/strong&gt; and produced by &lt;strong&gt;Merck&lt;/strong&gt; confirms that the product shown is &lt;strong&gt;Gardasil&lt;/strong&gt;, a vaccine used for preventing HPV-related diseases.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The scenario describes a structured approach where the patient learns to &lt;strong&gt;identify negative thoughts&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;challenge irrational beliefs&lt;/strong&gt;, and apply &lt;strong&gt;relaxation or coping techniques&lt;/strong&gt; during anxiety episodes. This method is characteristic of &lt;strong&gt;Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;CBT focuses on the connection between &lt;strong&gt;thoughts, feelings, and behaviors&lt;/strong&gt;, helping patients replace distorted thinking patterns with more realistic and constructive thoughts.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;For panic disorder, CBT is considered a &lt;strong&gt;first-line, evidence-based treatment&lt;/strong&gt; because it reduces fear responses, improves emotional regulation, and teaches long-term coping strategies.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Techniques used in CBT&amp;mdash;such as &lt;strong&gt;cognitive restructuring, relaxation training, exposure therapy, and breathing control&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;are specifically effective in reducing panic attacks and anticipatory anxiety.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the therapy described&amp;mdash;changing unhelpful thoughts and behaviors while practicing relaxation&amp;mdash;best matches &lt;strong&gt;Cognitive Behavioral Therapy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Interpersonal Therapy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Interpersonal Therapy (IPT) focuses on &lt;strong&gt;relationships, communication conflicts, grief, and social roles&lt;/strong&gt;. It does not specifically target panic symptoms or cognitive distortions.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Group Therapy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Group Therapy involves sharing experiences in a therapeutic group. While useful, it does &lt;strong&gt;not&lt;/strong&gt; focus on systematically correcting negative thought patterns.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Psychoanalytic Therapy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Psychoanalytic therapy explores &lt;strong&gt;unconscious conflicts&lt;/strong&gt;, early childhood experiences, and repressed emotions. It is not the primary method for treating panic disorder and does not teach structured coping skills.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Teaching a patient to &lt;strong&gt;challenge negative thoughts&lt;/strong&gt;, modify behavior, and practice relaxation aligns with the principles of &lt;strong&gt;Cognitive Behavioral Therapy (CBT)&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the most appropriate answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Supportive management, mainly ensuring a clear airway and proper breathing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Kerosene ingestion is a type of &lt;strong&gt;hydrocarbon poisoning&lt;/strong&gt;, and the greatest danger is &lt;strong&gt;aspiration into the lungs&lt;/strong&gt;, which can lead to severe complications such as &lt;strong&gt;chemical pneumonitis&lt;/strong&gt;, respiratory distress, and hypoxia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The child in the scenario already shows &lt;strong&gt;coughing and breathing difficulty&lt;/strong&gt;, which indicates early airway involvement. Therefore, the priority is to ensure a &lt;strong&gt;patent airway, adequate breathing, and sufficient oxygenation&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Supportive care includes providing &lt;strong&gt;oxygen therapy&lt;/strong&gt;, monitoring respiratory effort, checking lung sounds, maintaining airway patency, and preparing for advanced airway support if required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;No specific antidote exists for kerosene poisoning; thus, management is primarily focused on &lt;strong&gt;stabilizing the airway and respiratory status&lt;/strong&gt;, preventing aspiration, and observing for complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because respiratory compromise is the most life-threatening complication, &lt;strong&gt;airway and breathing management must always come first&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Give activated charcoal as an antidote&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Activated charcoal is &lt;strong&gt;not effective&lt;/strong&gt; for hydrocarbons like kerosene because it does not bind well to them. It also increases the risk of &lt;strong&gt;vomiting and aspiration&lt;/strong&gt;, worsening lung injury.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Make the child vomit right away to remove the kerosene&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Inducing vomiting is &lt;strongcontraindicated&lt; strong=&quot;&quot;&gt; because it greatly increases the risk of &lt;strong&gt;aspiration of kerosene into the lungs&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to chemical pneumonitis.&lt;/strongcontraindicated&lt;&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Do gastric lavage with normal saline&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Gastric lavage is &lt;strongnot strong=&quot;&quot;&gt; in kerosene ingestion for the same reason&amp;mdash;it increases aspiration risk. It is only considered in life-threatening amounts and with airway protection, which is rare.&lt;/strongnot&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because kerosene ingestion primarily causes &lt;strongrespiratory strong=&quot;&quot;&gt; through aspiration, the first and most important nursing action is &lt;strongensuring a=&quot;&quot; adequate=&quot;&quot; airway=&quot;&quot; and=&quot;&quot; patent=&quot;&quot; strong=&quot;&quot;&gt;, making supportive respiratory management the top priority.&lt;/strongensuring&gt;&lt;/strongrespiratory&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: West Bengal&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Shantiniketan is a renowned &lt;strong&gt;cultural, educational, and artistic center&lt;/strong&gt; founded by &lt;strong&gt;Rabindranath Tagore&lt;/strong&gt;, one of India&amp;rsquo;s greatest poets and the first Asian Nobel Laureate.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;It is located in the &lt;strong&gt;Birbhum district of West Bengal&lt;/strong&gt; and is known for its open-air schooling system, performing arts, visual arts, and Tagore&amp;rsquo;s philosophy of holistic learning.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Shantiniketan is home to &lt;strong&gt;Visva-Bharati University&lt;/strong&gt;, established by Tagore in 1921, which later became a Central University recognized for its rich heritage and contribution to Indian education and culture.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The institution is closely linked with Indian art, literature, music, and rural reconstruction, making it a cultural landmark in &lt;strong&gt;West Bengal&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on its origin, location, and historical significance, Shantiniketan is situated in the state of &lt;strong&gt;West Bengal&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Maharashtra&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Maharashtra is known for institutions like FTII and Tata Institute of Social Sciences, but Shantiniketan is not located there.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Gujarat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Gujarat has prominent cultural sites, but Shantiniketan was not founded or established in this state.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Uttar Pradesh&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Uttar Pradesh is associated with many historical landmarks, but Shantiniketan is not one of them.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Shantiniketan, established by &lt;strong&gt;Rabindranath Tagore&lt;/strong&gt;, is an iconic educational and cultural institution located in &lt;strong&gt;West Bengal&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct choice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Non-rebreather mask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Deep burns involving the &lt;strong&gt;face, neck, and upper chest&lt;/strong&gt; along with suspected &lt;strong&gt;inhalation injury&lt;/strong&gt; indicate a high risk of airway edema, carbon monoxide exposure, and impaired oxygen exchange.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In such emergencies, the priority is to deliver &lt;strong&gt;high-concentration oxygen (up to 95&amp;ndash;100%)&lt;/strong&gt; quickly to improve oxygenation and prevent respiratory failure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A &lt;strong&gt;non-rebreather mask (NRB)&lt;/strong&gt; is the best device for delivering the &lt;strong&gt;highest FiO₂&lt;/strong&gt; short of endotracheal intubation. It uses a reservoir bag and one-way valves to deliver near-pure oxygen while minimizing rebreathing of CO₂.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;NRB masks are commonly used in patients with &lt;strong&gt;inhalation burns, smoke inhalation, carbon monoxide poisoning, and acute hypoxia&lt;/strong&gt;, as they rapidly increase oxygen saturation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Additionally, the NRB system allows oxygen to be given with &lt;strong&gt;warm and humidified flow&lt;/strong&gt;, which helps reduce airway dryness and soot irritation in patients with inhalation injury.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Given the critical need for &lt;strong&gt;high FiO₂ and humidified oxygen therapy&lt;/strong&gt;, the non-rebreather mask is the most appropriate initial oxygen delivery device.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Oxygen tent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Provides cool, humidified oxygen but cannot deliver high FiO₂. Also impractical for severe respiratory distress or inhalation injury.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Nasal cannula&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Gives low-flow oxygen (up to 6 L/min) with a FiO₂ of only 24&amp;ndash;44%. Insufficient for severe hypoxia or inhalation burns.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Simple face mask&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Can deliver FiO₂ up to 60% but still inadequate compared to a non-rebreather mask, especially in critical burn injuries.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In a patient with &lt;strong&gt;facial and neck burns, suspected inhalation injury, and low oxygen saturation&lt;/strong&gt;, the &lt;strong&gt;non-rebreather mask&lt;/strong&gt; provides the most effective, rapid, and high-concentration oxygen support.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 1.5 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rotavirus vaccine (Rotarix), used under the &lt;strong&gt;Universal Immunization Programme (UIP)oral live attenuated vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; to protect infants from severe rotavirus gastroenteritis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The standard recommended quantity for each dose of &lt;strong&gt;Rotarix&lt;/strong&gt; is &lt;strong&gt;1.5 mL orally&lt;/strong&gt;. This dose ensures adequate antigen exposure to stimulate protective immunity.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The first dose is administered at &lt;strong&gt;6 weeks of age&lt;/strong&gt;, and the second dose is given at least &lt;strong&gt;4 weeks later&lt;/strong&gt;, following national immunization guidelines.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rotarix administration involves squeezing the prefilled oral applicator gently into the infant&amp;rsquo;s mouth, ensuring the entire &lt;strong&gt;1.5 mL dose&lt;/strong&gt; is swallowed for maximum vaccine effectiveness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on UIP recommendations and vaccine manufacturer guidelines, the usual amount given per dose of oral Rotarix is &lt;strong&gt;1.5 mL&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;0.5 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This volume is used for many injectable vaccines but is &lt;strong&gt;too small&lt;/strong&gt; for oral Rotarix, which requires a larger liquid dose.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;2.5 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is greater than the recommended dose and does &lt;strong&gt;not match&lt;/strong&gt; Rotarix formulation. No UIP schedule uses 2.5 mL for rotavirus vaccine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;0.1 mL&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This extremely small dose is used for tests like BCG or Mantoux but is &lt;strong&gt;not appropriate&lt;/strong&gt; for oral rotavirus vaccine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Under the &lt;strong&gt;Universal Immunization Programme&lt;/strong&gt;, each oral dose of &lt;strong&gt;Rotarix&lt;/strong&gt; is given as &lt;strong&gt;1.5 mL&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct and standard dose for infants.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: The hernia becomes firm, changes color, and the baby suddenly starts vomiting&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A reducible umbilical hernia is generally harmless in infants, but it can become dangerous if it progresses to &lt;strong&gt;incarceration or strangulation&lt;/strong&gt;. This occurs when a portion of the intestine becomes trapped, cutting off blood supply.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Signs such as the hernia becoming &lt;strong&gt;firm, discolored (red, purple, or dark), tender, or non-reducible&lt;/strong&gt; are classic warnings of strangulation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sudden onset of &lt;strong&gt;vomiting&lt;/strong&gt;, irritability, or crying indicates possible &lt;strong&gt;bowel obstruction or compromised blood flow&lt;/strong&gt;, which is a medical emergency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;If strangulation occurs, the infant can develop severe complications including &lt;strong&gt;necrosis of the bowel, sepsis, and shock&lt;/strong&gt;. Immediate medical intervention is required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, a change in firmness, color, and vomiting must be reported to the doctor immediately as it indicates a &lt;strong&gt;surgical emergency&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The hernia looks normal in color and the skin feels warm&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Warm skin is normal in infants, and a healthy umbilical hernia typically appears normal in color. This does not suggest danger.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The bulge becomes more noticeable when the baby cries&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Crying increases abdominal pressure, making the hernia more visible. This is a &lt;strong&gt;normal finding&lt;/strong&gt; in reducible hernias.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The hernia is soft and can be gently pushed back into the abdomen&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A soft, reducible hernia is considered &lt;strong&gt;normal and safe&lt;/strong&gt;. No emergency intervention is needed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A reducible umbilical hernia becomes an emergency when it turns &lt;strong&gt;firm, discolored, painful, or is associated with vomiting&lt;/strong&gt;. These signs indicate possible &lt;strong&gt;strangulation&lt;/strong&gt; and must be reported immediately.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;Rationale&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;The&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;Mini-Mental State Examination (MMSE)&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a standardized tool used to assess a patient&amp;rsquo;s cognitive function, including orientation, memory, attention, calculation, language, and visuospatial skills. The total possible score is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;30&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;According to the&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;provided MMSE interpretation table&lt;/span&gt;:&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;ul style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;24&amp;ndash;30&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;: No cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;19&amp;ndash;23&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;: Mild cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;10&amp;ndash;18&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;: Moderate cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;&amp;lt; 9&lt;/span&gt;&amp;nbsp;: Severe cognitive impairment&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;In this case, the patient has scored&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;19 out of 30&lt;/span&gt;. This score lies at the lower limit of the range&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;19&amp;ndash;23&lt;/span&gt;, which corresponds to&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;mild cognitive impairment&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;Although the score is close to the cutoff for moderate impairment, classification must be done strictly according to the defined ranges in the reference table. Therefore, the correct interpretation is&amp;nbsp;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;mild cognitive impairment&lt;/span&gt;.&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;p style=&quot;color: rgb(33, 37, 41); &quot;&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;font-weight: bolder;&quot;&gt;Hence, the correct answer is: Option 1 &amp;ndash; Mild cognitive impairment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/p&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Paris, France&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The United Nations Educational, Scientific and Cultural Organization (UNESCO) is a specialized agency of the United Nations that focuses on &lt;strong&gt;education, culture, science, and communication&lt;/strong&gt; to promote global peace and cooperation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;headquarters of UNESCO&lt;/strong&gt; is located in &lt;strong&gt;Paris, France&lt;/strong&gt;, where the organization was established in 1945 after World War II to build peace through cultural understanding and education.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;UNESCO&amp;rsquo;s main office in Paris hosts major global programs, executive meetings, research initiatives, and coordination of worldwide activities such as &lt;strong&gt;World Heritage Sites, literacy missions, and scientific collaboration&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Its location in Paris symbolizes the city&amp;rsquo;s long-standing tradition of supporting international cooperation, arts, culture, and diplomacy.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on historical and organizational facts, the headquarters of UNESCO is in &lt;strong&gt;Paris, France&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;New York, USA&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: New York is the headquarters of the &lt;strong&gt;United Nations (UN)&lt;/strong&gt;, not UNESCO.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Vienna, Austria&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Vienna hosts UN agencies such as the &lt;strong&gt;IAEA&lt;/strong&gt; (International Atomic Energy Agency) and UNODC, but not UNESCO.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Geneva, Switzerland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Geneva is home to &lt;strong&gt;WHO&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;UNHCR&lt;/strong&gt;, and other UN bodies, but not UNESCO headquarters.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The main office of UNESCO is located in &lt;strong&gt;Paris, France&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer based on United Nations organizational structure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Shah Jahan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Jama Masjid in Delhi, one of the &lt;strong&gt;largest and most iconic mosques in India&lt;/strong&gt;, was commissioned by the Mughal emperor &lt;strong&gt;Shah Jahan&lt;/strong&gt;, who is widely known for his grand architectural creations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Construction of the Jama Masjid began in &lt;strong&gt;1650&lt;/strong&gt; and was completed in &lt;strong&gt;1656&lt;/strong&gt;, reflecting the architectural magnificence and craftsmanship characteristic of Shah Jahan&amp;rsquo;s reign.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Shah Jahan is famous for building several monumental structures, including the &lt;strong&gt;Taj Mahal&lt;/strong&gt; and the &lt;strong&gt;Red Fort&lt;/strong&gt;, making him one of the greatest patrons of Mughal architecture.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The Jama Masjid stands as a major religious and historical landmark, known for its vast courtyard, red sandstone and marble design, and its cultural significance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the Mughal ruler responsible for building Delhi&amp;rsquo;s Jama Masjid is &lt;strong&gt;Shah Jahan&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Akbar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Akbar built architectural masterpieces like &lt;strong&gt;Fatehpur Sikri&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Buland Darwaza&lt;/strong&gt;, but he did not build the Jama Masjid of Delhi.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Aurangzeb&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Aurangzeb built the &lt;strong&gt;Badshahi Mosque&lt;/strong&gt; in Lahore and other structures, but the Delhi Jama Masjid was completed before his reign.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Jahangir&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Jahangir was more focused on gardens and arts; he did not undertake construction of the Jama Masjid.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Delhi&amp;rsquo;s Jama Masjid, a masterpiece of Mughal architecture, was built under the command of &lt;strong&gt;Shah Jahan&lt;/strong&gt;, making him the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Sarojini Naidu&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sarojini Naidu, also known as the &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;Nightingale of India&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;, holds the distinction of being the &lt;strong&gt;first woman governor of any Indian state&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;She was appointed as the &lt;strong&gt;Governor of Uttar Pradesh&lt;/strong&gt; in 1947 and served until her death in 1949, making her the first woman to occupy such a high constitutional position in independent India.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Her appointment reflected her strong contributions to India&amp;rsquo;s freedom struggle, leadership qualities, and significant role in national politics.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sarojini Naidu was also the first Indian woman to become the &lt;strong&gt;President of the Indian National Congress&lt;/strong&gt;, further highlighting her pioneering role in Indian history.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, based on historical significance and official records, Sarojini Naidu is recognized as the first woman governor in India.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Indira Gandhi&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Indira Gandhi was the &lt;strong&gt;first woman Prime Minister of India&lt;/strong&gt;, not a state governor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Vijayalakshmi Pandit&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: She was the first woman to become the &lt;strong&gt;President of the UN General Assembly&lt;/strong&gt;, but she was never a governor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sucheta Kripalani&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Sucheta Kripalani was the first woman &lt;strong&gt;Chief Minister&lt;/strong&gt; (Uttar Pradesh), not a governor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The first woman to serve as a state governor in India was &lt;strong&gt;Sarojini Naidu&lt;/strong&gt;, making her the correct and historically important answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Active phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The first stage of labor is divided into three phases: &lt;strong&gt;latent, active, and transition&lt;/strong&gt;. These phases are distinguished by the degree of cervical dilation, the strength of contractions, and the mother&amp;rsquo;s labor progress.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A cervical dilation of &lt;strong&gt;5 cm&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;80% effacement&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;strong, regular contractions every 3&amp;ndash;4 minutes&lt;/strong&gt; clearly falls within the &lt;strong&gt;active phase&lt;/strong&gt; of labor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The active phase typically spans from &lt;strong&gt;4 cm to 7 cm dilation&lt;/strong&gt;, characterized by more rapid cervical change and stronger, closer contractions that facilitate fetal descent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Women in the active phase require close monitoring of contractions, fetal heart rate, pain management, and preparation for the upcoming transition phase.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on cervical dilation and contraction pattern, the woman is in the &lt;strong&gt;active phase&lt;/strong&gt; of the first stage of labor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Latent phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The latent phase usually includes slow cervical dilation from &lt;strong&gt;0 to 3&amp;ndash;4 cm&lt;/strong&gt;. Contractions are mild to moderate and occur less frequently compared to this scenario.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Transition phase&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The transition phase occurs at &lt;strong&gt;8&amp;ndash;10 cm dilation&lt;/strong&gt; and is the most intense phase. Since the patient is only 5 cm dilated, this phase does not apply.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Second stage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The second stage begins at full dilation (10 cm) and ends with birth. The patient is not yet fully dilated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;With &lt;strong&gt;5 cm dilation, 80% effacement, and strong contractions every 3&amp;ndash;4 minutes&lt;/strong&gt;, the woman is correctly identified as being in the &lt;strong&gt;active phase&lt;/strong&gt; of the first &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Frequent or constant swallowing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After a &lt;strong&gt;tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy&lt;/strong&gt;, the biggest postoperative risk is &lt;strong&gt;hemorrhage&lt;/strong&gt;, which can occur immediately or even several days after surgery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Children often cannot verbalize active bleeding, so they show signs such as &lt;strong&gt;frequent or continuous swallowing&lt;/strong&gt; as they attempt to clear blood draining down the throat.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;persistent swallowing&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; is one of the earliest and most reliable indicators of postoperative bleeding, even before visible blood appears in the mouth.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Bleeding after tonsil surgery can be &lt;strong&gt;rapid and dangerous&lt;/strong&gt;, leading to airway obstruction, aspiration, or hypovolemic shock. Therefore, parents must report this symptom &lt;strong&gt;immediately&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Prompt recognition and treatment are essential to prevent severe complications, making &lt;strong&gt;frequent swallowing&lt;/strong&gt; a critical warning sign.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Mild fever around 99.5&amp;deg;F (37.5&amp;deg;C)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A slight increase in temperature is &lt;strong&gt;normal&lt;/strong&gt; after surgery and not an indication of bleeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;White patches seen in the throat&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: White or yellowish scabs are a &lt;strong&gt;normal part of healing&lt;/strong&gt; after tonsillectomy and do not indicate infection or bleeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Complaints of throat discomfort&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Pain or discomfort is expected for several days after the procedure. It does not indicate bleeding unless accompanied by other symptoms.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Among the discharge warning signs, &lt;strong&gt;frequent or constant swallowing&lt;/strong&gt; is the most important to report because it strongly suggests &lt;strong&gt;postoperative bleeding&lt;/strong&gt; following tonsillectomy and adenoidectomy.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Akathisia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Haloperidol is a typical antipsychotic that often causes &lt;strong&gt;extrapyramidal side effects (EPS)&lt;/strong&gt;. One of the most common early-onset EPS is &lt;strong&gt;akathisia&lt;/strong&gt;, a condition characterized by an intense feeling of inner restlessness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Akathisia presents with symptoms such as an inability to sit still, constant movement of the legs, pacing, and an overwhelming urge to keep moving. Patients often describe it as feeling &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;restless inside&lt;/strong&gt;,&amp;rdquo; exactly as stated in the scenario.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This condition typically arises within &lt;strong&gt;days to weeks&lt;/strong&gt; after starting or increasing the dose of antipsychotics like haloperidol. It can cause significant distress and may lead to non-adherence to treatment if not addressed promptly.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Management includes reducing the dose, switching antipsychotics, or adding medications such as &lt;strong&gt;beta-blockers (propranolol), benzodiazepines, or anticholinergics&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Since the described symptoms match the classic clinical picture, the side effect is identified as &lt;strong&gt;akathisia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dystonia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Dystonia involves &lt;strong&gt;painful, sustained muscle contractions&lt;/strong&gt; such as neck twisting (torticollis), eye deviation, or tongue protrusion. It does not cause inner restlessness or pacing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Pseudoparkinsonism&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This EPS resembles Parkinson&amp;rsquo;s disease, with symptoms like &lt;strong&gt;mask-like face, tremor, rigidity, shuffling gait&lt;/strong&gt;, and bradykinesia. Restlessness is not a key feature.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Tardive dyskinesia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Tardive dyskinesia is a &lt;strong&gt;late-onset&lt;/strong&gt; side effect involving &lt;strong&gt;involuntary movements&lt;/strong&gt; such as lip smacking, tongue thrusting, and chewing motions. It does not manifest as restlessness or inability to stay still.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The patient&amp;rsquo;s symptoms of &lt;strong&gt;inner restlessness, inability to keep still, and constant leg movement&lt;/strong&gt; strongly indicate &lt;strong&gt;akathisia&lt;/strong&gt;, a common EPS associated with haloperidol.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: To help deliver the after-coming head in a breech birth&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Burns&amp;ndash;Marshall maneuver&lt;/strong&gt; is a well-known obstetric technique specifically used during a &lt;strong&gt;breech delivery&lt;/strong&gt; to assist in delivering the &lt;strong&gt;after-coming head&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this maneuver, after the baby&amp;rsquo;s body has been delivered in a breech birth, the infant is allowed to hang momentarily to provide gentle downward traction by gravity. This helps flex the fetal head naturally.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Once the head begins to appear at the perineum, the practitioner applies controlled upward traction to complete delivery. This helps prevent complications such as &lt;strong&gt;entrapment of the after-coming head&lt;/strong&gt;, which is a major risk in breech births.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The goal of the Burns&amp;ndash;Marshall maneuver is to maintain correct alignment of the fetal head, minimize trauma, and ensure a safe vaginal breech delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, this maneuver is used specifically for assisting the &lt;strong&gt;delivery of the after-coming head&lt;/strong&gt; during a breech presentation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To assist with removal of the placenta&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Placental delivery involves controlled cord traction (Brandt&amp;ndash;Andrews maneuver), not the Burns&amp;ndash;Marshall maneuver.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To control postpartum bleeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Management of postpartum hemorrhage includes uterotonics, uterine massage, and surgical interventions&amp;mdash;not this maneuver.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;To correct shoulder dystocia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Shoulder dystocia is managed with maneuvers like &lt;strong&gt;McRoberts&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;suprapubic pressure&lt;/strong&gt;, or &lt;strong&gt;Woods&amp;rsquo; screw maneuver&lt;/strong&gt;, not Burns&amp;ndash;Marshall.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Burns&amp;ndash;Marshall maneuver&lt;/strong&gt; is exclusively used to assist delivery of the &lt;strong&gt;after-coming head in a breech birth&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct choice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Fundal pressure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The description indicates that the healthcare provider is pressing &lt;strong&gt;firmly downward on the top of the uterus&lt;/strong&gt;, also called the &lt;strong&gt;fundus&lt;/strong&gt;. This action is known as &lt;strong&gt;fundal pressure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Fundal pressure is applied during the second stage of labor with the intention of assisting fetal descent by exerting manual pressure from above the uterus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;However, this technique is &lt;strong&gt;not routinely recommended&lt;/strong&gt; because it can increase the risk of complications such as &lt;strong&gt;uterine rupture, shoulder dystocia worsening, or fetal injury&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The key identifying feature in the image is the provider&amp;rsquo;s hands pushing down on the uterus during labor, which fits the definition of &lt;strong&gt;fundal pressure&lt;/strong&gt; and none of the specialized obstetric maneuvers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, the procedure being demonstrated is correctly recognized as &lt;strong&gt;fundal pressure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Loveset maneuver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The Loveset maneuver is used during breech delivery to help deliver the fetal arms. It does not involve pressing on the uterus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;McRoberts maneuver&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: The McRoberts maneuver involves flexing the mother&amp;#39;s thighs tightly onto her abdomen to relieve &lt;strong&gt;shoulder dystocia&lt;/strong&gt;. No uterine pressure is applied.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;None of the above&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This is incorrect because the described action clearly matches the definition of &lt;strong&gt;fundal pressure&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Pressing firmly downward on the uterine fundus is known as &lt;strong&gt;fundal pressure&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct interpretation of the procedure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Kawasaki disease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The symptoms described&amp;mdash;&lt;strong&gt;high fever for 5 days&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;red eyes without discharge&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;strawberry tongue&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;widespread rash&lt;/strong&gt;, and &lt;strong&gt;red/swollen hands and feet&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;strongly indicate &lt;strong&gt;Kawasaki disease&lt;/strong&gt;, a type of acute systemic vasculitis that primarily affects children under 5 years of age.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Kawasaki disease typically presents with prolonged fever (&amp;ge;5 days) along with at least four of the following: &lt;strong&gt;bilateral non-purulent conjunctivitis&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;oral changes&lt;/strong&gt; such as strawberry tongue or cracked lips, &lt;strong&gt;polymorphous rash&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;extremity changes&lt;/strong&gt; like swelling or redness, and &lt;strong&gt;cervical lymphadenopathy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The red, swollen extremities and strawberry tongue are highly characteristic features, and the absence of eye discharge suggests inflammatory&amp;mdash;not infectious&amp;mdash;conjunctivitis, further supporting Kawasaki disease.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Kawasaki disease is clinically important because untreated cases can lead to serious complications such as &lt;strong&gt;coronary artery aneurysms&lt;/strong&gt;. Early recognition and treatment with &lt;strong&gt;IVIG and aspirin&lt;/strong&gt; greatly reduce cardiovascular risks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the combination of prolonged fever plus mucocutaneous and extremity changes points clearly to &lt;strong&gt;Kawasaki disease&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Measles (rubeola)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Measles causes cough, coryza, conjunctivitis with discharge, Koplik spots, and a descending rash. Swollen hands/feet and strawberry tongue are not typical findings.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Scarlet fever&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Scarlet fever features strawberry tongue and a sandpaper-like rash, but it does not cause red, swollen hands/feet or conjunctivitis without discharge.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hand, foot, and mouth disease&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: HFMD causes painful ulcers and vesicles on hands, feet, and mouth&amp;mdash;not strawberry tongue, prolonged fever, or conjunctivitis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The classic constellation of &lt;strong&gt;fever, strawberry tongue, non-purulent conjunctivitis, rash, and extremity swelling&lt;/strong&gt; is diagnostic of &lt;strong&gt;Kawasaki disease&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Perform a sterile in-and-out urethral catheterization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;For a &lt;strong&gt;15-month-old child who is not toilet trained&lt;/strong&gt;, the most reliable and accurate way to obtain a &lt;strong&gt;sterile urine sample for culture&lt;/strong&gt; is through &lt;strong&gt;sterile in-and-out urethral catheterization&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Catheterization avoids contamination from skin flora, diapers, or perineal bacteria, giving a sample appropriate for diagnosing &lt;strong&gt;urinary tract infections (UTIs)&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The specimen collected is directly from the bladder, ensuring that it meets strict requirements for &lt;strong&gt;sterility and accuracy&lt;/strong&gt;, which is essential for cultures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Other methods such as urine bags and diaper-based collection often produce contaminated samples, leading to &lt;strong&gt;false-positive results&lt;/strong&gt; or repeat testing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, catheterization is considered the &lt;strong&gt;gold standard&lt;/strong&gt; for obtaining a sterile urine specimen in non&amp;ndash;toilet-trained children.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Use cotton balls in the diaper to absorb urine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Cotton balls are &lt;strong&gt;not sterile&lt;/strong&gt; and absorb urine mixed with skin bacteria. This method is not acceptable for culture testing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Attach a urine collection bag to the child&amp;rsquo;s perineum&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Urine bags have a &lt;strong&gt;high contamination rate (up to 60%)&lt;/strong&gt;. They are acceptable only for urinalysis, not for culture.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Try to catch a clean mid-stream sample in a sterile cup&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A 15-month-old child cannot reliably provide a clean mid-stream sample, and contamination is very likely.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The most accurate and sterile method for collecting urine from a non&amp;ndash;toilet-trained child is &lt;strong&gt;in-and-out urethral catheterization&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the preferred choice for urine culture.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Ask directly if they are thinking about suicide and whether they have a plan&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A sudden shift from depression to &lt;strong&gt;cheerfulness or calmness&lt;/strong&gt; after a suicide attempt can be a dangerous sign. It may indicate that the patient has &lt;strong&gt;made a decision to attempt suicide again&lt;/strong&gt; or has already formed a plan, which can temporarily relieve inner conflict and appear as improved mood.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because this improvement may be a sign of increased suicide risk, the nurse&amp;rsquo;s first and most important action is to &lt;strong&gt;directly assess suicidal thoughts, intent, and the presence of a plan&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Asking directly about suicide is not harmful&amp;mdash;it is a &lt;strong&gt;therapeutic, evidence-based intervention&lt;/strong&gt; and helps prevent further harm. Patients often feel relieved when someone acknowledges their emotional state openly.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Assessment should include questions about &lt;strong&gt;current suicidal ideation, specific plans, access to means, and readiness to act&lt;/strong&gt;. This allows the nurse to determine the level of risk and initiate immediate safety measures.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, in a patient showing sudden mood improvement after a suicide attempt, the priority is a direct, immediate suicide risk assessment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Note the patient&amp;rsquo;s improved mood in the chart&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Documentation is important, but it is &lt;strong&gt;not the first priority&lt;/strong&gt;. Immediate safety assessment takes precedence.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Praise the patient for feeling better&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Praising may discourage the patient from sharing suicidal thoughts and may falsely validate dangerous behavior. It also ignores the warning signs.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Ask about their plans for getting a new job&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This shifts focus away from the critical issue and may miss an urgent suicide risk. Psychosocial questions can wait until safety is ensured.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A sudden positive change in mood after a suicide attempt is a red flag. The immediate priority is to &lt;strong&gt;ask directly about suicidal thoughts and plans&lt;/strong&gt; to ensure the patient&amp;rsquo;s safety.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Reactive NST &amp;ndash; baby is doing well&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A Non-Stress Test (NST) is used to evaluate &lt;strong&gt;fetal well-being&lt;/strong&gt; by assessing fetal heart rate patterns in response to fetal movement. A healthy fetus should demonstrate accelerations that reflect adequate oxygenation and neurological function.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A &amp;ldquo;reactive&amp;rdquo; NST is defined as &lt;strong&gt;two or more accelerations&lt;/strong&gt; of at least &lt;strong&gt;15 beats per minute above baseline&lt;/strong&gt; lasting for at least &lt;strong&gt;15 seconds&lt;/strong&gt; within a &lt;strong&gt;20-minute&lt;/strong&gt; window for pregnancies &amp;ge;32 weeks.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this case, the NST shows a baseline fetal heart rate of &lt;strong&gt;140 bpm&lt;/strong&gt; (normal), &lt;strong&gt;moderate variability&lt;/strong&gt; (good indicator of fetal oxygenation), and &lt;strong&gt;three accelerations&lt;/strong&gt; that meet the 15&amp;times;15 criteria.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The presence of adequate accelerations and normal variability confirms that the fetus is &lt;strong&gt;well-oxygenated and neurologically responsive&lt;/strong&gt;, which defines a &lt;strong&gt;reactive NST&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A reactive NST indicates the baby is doing well, and no further immediate testing is required unless other clinical concerns are present.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Non-reactive NST &amp;ndash; further testing needed&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A non-reactive NST lacks the required accelerations. This patient has three accelerations meeting criteria, so the NST is &lt;strong&gt;reactive, not non-reactive&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Positive NST &amp;ndash; indicates fetal distress&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: &amp;ldquo;Positive&amp;rdquo; is a term used for the &lt;strong&gt;Contraction Stress Test (CST)&lt;/strong&gt;, not NST. NST results are interpreted as &lt;strong&gt;reactive or non-reactive&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Sinusoidal pattern &amp;ndash; suggests severe fetal anemia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: A sinusoidal pattern is smooth, wavelike, and absent of variability or accelerations. This fetus has &lt;strong&gt;normal variability and accelerations&lt;/strong&gt;, so this pattern is not present.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;With &lt;strong&gt;moderate variability&lt;/strong&gt;, a normal baseline, and &lt;strong&gt;three accelerations&lt;/strong&gt; meeting the 15&amp;times;15 rule, the NST is clearly &lt;strong&gt;reactive&lt;/strong&gt;, indicating the baby is &lt;strong&gt;healthy and well-oxygenated&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Cervical cancer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cervavac is an &lt;strong&gt;Indian-made Human Papillomavirus (HPV) vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; developed to prevent infections caused by high-risk HPV types that are strongly linked to &lt;strong&gt;cervical cancer&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Persistent infection with high-risk HPV strains such as &lt;strong&gt;HPV-16 and HPV-18&lt;/strong&gt; is responsible for nearly &lt;strong&gt;70% of cervical cancer cases&lt;/strong&gt; worldwide. Cervavac helps build immunity against these oncogenic strains.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The vaccine is recommended for adolescents and young adults to protect against precancerous cervical lesions and reduce the long-term risk of &lt;strong&gt;cervical malignancy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cervavac is included in India&amp;rsquo;s vaccination initiatives to reduce cervical cancer burden, making it a vital public health tool.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the primary purpose of the Cervavac vaccine is the &lt;strong&gt;prevention of cervical cancer&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Liver cancer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Liver cancer is primarily prevented by the &lt;strong&gt;Hepatitis B vaccine&lt;/strong&gt;, not HPV vaccines.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Lung cancer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: There is no vaccine that prevents lung cancer; it is mainly related to smoking and environmental exposure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Breast cancer&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: No vaccine currently prevents breast cancer. Cervavac specifically targets HPV-related cancers.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cervavac is an HPV vaccine designed to protect against &lt;strong&gt;cervical cancer&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct choice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Saheli (Ormeloxifene)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Saheli, also known as &lt;strong&gt;Ormeloxifene&lt;/strong&gt;, is a non-hormonal oral contraceptive pill widely used in India. One of its unique advantages is that it is taken &lt;strong&gt;once a week&lt;/strong&gt;, making it suitable for clients who prefer a method that does not require daily dosing.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This weekly dosing schedule helps improve compliance, especially among individuals who may find it difficult to remember daily pills.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Saheli works by altering the endometrium and cervical mucus, preventing implantation and making pregnancy less likely.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because the client specifically wants a contraceptive method that is taken &lt;strong&gt;weekly&lt;/strong&gt; and not daily, Saheli (Ormeloxifene) is the correct option.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Other contraceptive pills require &lt;strong&gt;daily intake&lt;/strong&gt; or are used only in emergencies, making them unsuitable for the client&amp;rsquo;s preference.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Progestin-only pill&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Progestin-only pills (mini-pills) must be taken &lt;strong&gt;every day at the same time&lt;/strong&gt; to be effective. They are not weekly pills.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Combined oral contraceptive pill&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Combined oral contraceptives also require &lt;strong&gt;daily intake&lt;/strong&gt; and therefore do not match the client&amp;rsquo;s preference for a weekly method.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Emergency contraceptive pill&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Emergency contraceptives are taken &lt;strong&gt;only after unprotected intercourse&lt;/strong&gt; and are not meant for routine weekly use.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Among all available contraceptive options, &lt;strong&gt;Saheli (Ormeloxifene)&lt;/strong&gt; is the only pill taken &lt;strong&gt;once a week&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct choice for a client seeking a non-daily birth-control method.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Distributive shock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Anaphylactic shock is a life-threatening form of &lt;strong&gt;severe allergic reaction&lt;/strong&gt; caused by massive release of chemical mediators such as &lt;strong&gt;histamine, leukotrienes, and prostaglandins&lt;/strong&gt; from mast cells and basophils.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;These mediators result in profound &lt;strong&gt;vasodilation&lt;/strong&gt; and increased &lt;strong&gt;capillary permeability&lt;/strong&gt;, causing fluid to leak out of blood vessels into the tissues, leading to &lt;strong&gt;relative hypovolemia&lt;/strong&gt; and severe hypotension.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Because the primary problem in anaphylaxis is &lt;strong&gt;maldistribution of blood flow&lt;/strong&gt; rather than actual blood loss or pump failure, it is classified under &lt;strong&gt;distributive shock&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Distributive shock includes three main types: &lt;strong&gt;septic shock, neurogenic shock, and anaphylactic shock&lt;/strong&gt;, all characterized by widespread vasodilation and altered vascular tone.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, based on its pathophysiology&amp;mdash;massive vasodilation and fluid shift&amp;mdash;anaphylactic shock is correctly categorized as &lt;strong&gt;distributive shock&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cardiogenic shock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Cardiogenic shock occurs due to &lt;strong&gt;pump failure&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., myocardial infarction). Anaphylaxis does not involve heart pump failure as the primary issue.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hypovolemic shock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hypovolemic shock results from &lt;strong&gt;actual loss of blood or fluids&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., hemorrhage, dehydration). In anaphylaxis, the volume loss is &lt;strong&gt;relative&lt;/strong&gt;, due to vasodilation, not true loss.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Obstructive shock&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Obstructive shock occurs when blood flow is physically blocked (e.g., pulmonary embolism, cardiac tamponade). This mechanism does not apply to anaphylaxis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Anaphylactic shock involves &lt;strong&gt;widespread vasodilation and increased capillary permeability&lt;/strong&gt;, making it a classic example of &lt;strong&gt;distributive shock&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Inform the surgeon right away&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After a &lt;strong&gt;Coronary Artery Bypass Grafting (CABG)postoperative hemorrhage&lt;/strong&gt; or surgical complications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The expected chest tube output in the early postoperative period is generally &lt;strong&gt;50&amp;ndash;100 mL/hour&lt;/strong&gt;. Drainage of &lt;strong&gt;300 mL in 1 hour&lt;/strong&gt; is dangerously high and suggests possible internal bleeding or failure of surgical hemostasis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Massive or rapidly increasing drainage is a red flag for complications such as &lt;strong&gt;cardiac tamponade, hemorrhage, or graft failure&lt;/strong&gt;, all requiring immediate surgical evaluation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Delaying action can quickly lead to &lt;strong&gt;hypovolemic shock&lt;/strong&gt;, hemodynamic instability, and life-threatening deterioration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the nurse must &lt;strong&gt;notify the surgeon immediately&lt;/strong&gt; so that prompt interventions&amp;mdash;such as returning the patient to the operating room&amp;mdash;can be initiated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Record it as normal drainage&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This amount is &lt;strong&gt;not normal&lt;/strong&gt;. Recording without action would delay life-saving intervention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Clamp the chest tube to slow the bleeding&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Clamping a chest tube can cause &lt;strong&gt;tension pneumothorax&lt;/strong&gt;, tamponade, and worsening of intrathoracic pressure. It is &lt;strong&gt;never done&lt;/strong&gt; for active bleeding.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Give a prescribed diuretic immediately&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Diuretics do not stop surgical bleeding and may worsen hemodynamic instability by reducing circulating volume.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Chest tube drainage of &lt;strong&gt;300 mL in 1 hour&lt;/strong&gt; after CABG indicates a potential surgical emergency. The nurse must &lt;strong&gt;notify the surgeon immediately&lt;/strong&gt; to prevent rapid deterioration.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Opaque&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The word &lt;strong&gt;transparent&lt;/strong&gt; means &amp;ldquo;allowing light to pass through so that objects behind can be clearly seen.&amp;rdquo; It describes something clear, see-through, or easily understood.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The opposite of transparent is &lt;strong&gt;opaque&lt;/strong&gt;, which means &amp;ldquo;not allowing light to pass through&amp;rdquo; and refers to something that cannot be seen through.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Opaque materials or objects block visibility, making them the direct contrast to transparency.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the word that expresses the opposite meaning of transparent is &lt;strong&gt;opaque&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Clear&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: &amp;ldquo;Clear&amp;rdquo; is similar in meaning to transparent, not the opposite.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Lucid&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: &amp;ldquo;Lucid&amp;rdquo; means easy to understand or mentally clear. It does not refer to visibility and is not the opposite of transparent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Translucent&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: &amp;ldquo;Translucent&amp;rdquo; means partially see-through, allowing some light to pass but not detailed images. It is not the opposite of transparent.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Among the given options, &lt;strong&gt;opaque&lt;/strong&gt; is the true opposite of &lt;strong&gt;transparent&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Monochorionic&amp;ndash;diamniotic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In twin pregnancies, placentation is determined by the number of &lt;strong&gt;chorions (placentas)&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;amnions (sacs)&lt;/strong&gt;. An ultrasound showing &lt;strong&gt;one shared placenta&lt;/strong&gt; indicates that the pregnancy is &lt;strong&gt;monochorionic&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The presence of a &lt;strong&gt;thin membrane&lt;/strong&gt; separating the two fetuses means each baby has its own &lt;strong&gt;amniotic sac&lt;/strong&gt;. When twins share one placenta but are separated by a membrane, the pregnancy is &lt;strong&gt;diamniotic&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, one chorion + two amnions = &lt;strong&gt;Monochorionic&amp;ndash;diamniotic (MCDA)&lt;/strong&gt; twins.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;MCDA is the most common form of identical twinning and carries risks such as &lt;strong&gt;twin-to-twin transfusion syndrome (TTTS)&lt;/strong&gt;, making accurate identification clinically important.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Therefore, the ultrasound findings of a single placenta with a thin dividing membrane match the pattern of &lt;strong&gt;monochorionic&amp;ndash;diamniotic&lt;/strong&gt; placentation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Dichorionic&amp;ndash;diamniotic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Di&amp;ndash;Di twins have &lt;strong&gt;two placentas&lt;/strong&gt; (or one thick fused placenta) and a thicker separating membrane. The question states &lt;strong&gt;one shared placenta&lt;/strong&gt;, ruling this out.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Monochorionic&amp;ndash;monoamniotic&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Mo&amp;ndash;Mo twins share both the &lt;strong&gt;placenta and the amniotic sac&lt;/strong&gt;, so there is &lt;strong&gt;no membrane&lt;/strong&gt; between them. The presence of a membrane excludes this option.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Conjoined twins&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Conjoined twins are always &lt;strong&gt;monoamniotic&lt;/strong&gt; with no separating membrane. The image shows a membrane, making this incorrect.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;A single placenta plus a thin membrane separating the fetuses is diagnostic of &lt;strong&gt;monochorionic&amp;ndash;diamniotic&lt;/strong&gt; twins.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: Bladder exstrophy&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Bladder exstrophy is a rare congenital defect in which the &lt;strong&gt;bladder develops outside the body&lt;/strong&gt; due to incomplete closure of the lower abdominal wall.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;In this condition, the bladder appears &lt;strong&gt;exposed, bright red, and turned outward&lt;/strong&gt; on the lower abdomen, and urine drains directly onto the skin because the bladder cannot store urine internally.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The defect is usually associated with abnormalities of the &lt;strong&gt;urethra, genitalia, and pubic bones&lt;/strong&gt;. Immediate protection of the exposed bladder with a &lt;strong&gt;sterile, non-adherent moist dressing&lt;/strong&gt; is essential to prevent infection and dryness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Definitive treatment involves surgical reconstruction to restore normal urinary and bladder function.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The findings described in the question&amp;mdash;exposed bladder on the lower abdomen with urine leaking onto the skin&amp;mdash;are classic hallmarks of &lt;strong&gt;bladder exstrophy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Cryptorchidism&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Cryptorchidism refers to &lt;strong&gt;undescended testes&lt;/strong&gt;. It does not involve the bladder and has no external urine leakage.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Epispadias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Epispadias is an abnormal opening of the urethra on the &lt;strong&gt;dorsal (upper) side&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Hypospadias&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: Hypospadias is a urethral opening on the &lt;strong&gt;ventral (underside)&lt;/strong&gt; of the penis. The bladder remains internal and normal.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The presence of an &lt;strong&gt;externally exposed bladder&lt;/strong&gt; with urine leaking onto the abdomen identifies the condition as &lt;strong&gt;bladder exstrophy&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Correct Answer: 3 km&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;This is a basic displacement (straight-line distance) problem using simple coordinate movement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The person first walks &lt;strong&gt;2 km north&lt;/strong&gt; and then &lt;strong&gt;2 km south&lt;/strong&gt;. These two movements cancel each other out, bringing the person back to the original north&amp;ndash;south level.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;The only movement that does not cancel out is the &lt;strong&gt;3 km east&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Since the final position is directly east of the starting point, the displacement from the starting point is simply the horizontal distance: &lt;strong&gt;3 km&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Thus, the straight-line distance from the starting point to the final point is &lt;strong&gt;3 km&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;4 km&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This would be true only if the north and south distances were unequal, but here they cancel out exactly.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;5 km&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: This would imply a right-triangle path, but since vertical movement cancels, no triangle is formed.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;7 km&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;Rationale: 7 km is the total path walked, not the displacement from starting point.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span style=&quot;&quot;&gt;After opposite north and south movements cancel out, only the &lt;strong&gt;3 km east&lt;/strong&gt; shift remains. Therefore, the person ends up &lt;strong&gt;3 km&lt;/strong&gt; away from the starting point.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Physostigmine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Severe anticholinergic poisoning presents with the classic signs: &lt;strong&gt;confusion, tachycardia, mydriasis (dilated pupils), dry skin, urinary retention, and delirium&lt;/strong&gt;. These symptoms result from excessive blockade of &lt;strong&gt;acetylcholine&lt;/strong&gt; at both central and peripheral muscarinic receptors.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Physostigmine&lt;/strong&gt; is a &lt;strong&gt;centrally acting acetylcholinesterase inhibitor&lt;/strong&gt; that crosses the &lt;strong&gt;blood&amp;ndash;brain barrier&lt;/strong&gt;. By inhibiting acetylcholinesterase, it increases acetylcholine levels, effectively reversing both &lt;strong&gt;CNS and peripheral&lt;/strong&gt; anticholinergic effects.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;It is the &lt;strong&gt;antidote of choice&lt;/strong&gt; for life-threatening anticholinergic toxicity when severe agitation, hallucinations, or delirium is present.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Examples of substances causing anticholinergic toxicity include: &lt;strong&gt;atropine, diphenhydramine, tricyclic antidepressants, scopolamine, and antispasmodics&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Because the patient has severe central and peripheral symptoms, a centrally acting antidote&amp;mdash;&lt;strong&gt;physostigmine&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;is required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Naloxone&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Naloxone is an opioid antagonist used for &lt;strong&gt;opioid overdose&lt;/strong&gt;, not anticholinergic poisoning. It has no effect on acetylcholine pathways.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pralidoxime&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Pralidoxime is used for &lt;strong&gt;organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning&lt;/strong&gt;, where acetylcholine is excessively high. It is not appropriate for anticholinergic toxicity.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Atropine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Atropine itself is a &lt;strong&gt;potent anticholinergic drug&lt;/strong&gt; and would worsen the patient&amp;rsquo;s condition, not treat it.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Only &lt;strong&gt;physostigmine&lt;/strong&gt; reverses both central and peripheral anticholinergic toxicity by increasing acetylcholine levels in the brain and body, making it the correct antidote.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: High blood pressure, slow pulse, and irregular breathing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad&lt;/strong&gt; is a late and life-threatening sign of &lt;strong&gt;increased intracranial pressure (ICP)&lt;/strong&gt;, often seen in severe head injury. It reflects the brain&amp;rsquo;s response to rising pressure and impending brain herniation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The three classic components of Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad are:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Hypertension (&amp;uarr; systolic BP)&lt;/strong&gt; with widening pulse pressure &amp;mdash; the body increases blood pressure to maintain cerebral perfusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Bradycardia (&amp;darr; pulse)&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;mdash; a vagal response caused by pressure on the brainstem.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Irregular breathing&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;mdash; due to brainstem compression affecting respiratory centers. This may appear as &lt;strong&gt;Cheyne&amp;ndash;Stokes respirations, Biot&amp;rsquo;s respirations, or ataxic breathing&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Therefore, the correct combination matching Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad is: &lt;strong&gt;high BP + slow pulse + irregular breathing&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This pattern signals a neurological emergency requiring immediate intervention to prevent herniation and death.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Low blood pressure, fast pulse, and Kussmaul breathing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: This pattern indicates &lt;strong&gt;shock or metabolic acidosis&lt;/strong&gt; (e.g., diabetic ketoacidosis), not increased ICP.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;High blood pressure, fast pulse, and Cheyne&amp;ndash;Stokes breathing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Although Cheyne&amp;ndash;Stokes can occur with ICP elevation, the pulse would be &lt;strong&gt;slow&lt;/strong&gt;, not fast. So this does not match Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Low blood pressure, slow pulse, and apnea&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: This suggests terminal deterioration or severe hypoxia, not the characteristic pattern of Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Cushing&amp;rsquo;s triad&lt;/strong&gt; presents with &lt;strong&gt;hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations&lt;/strong&gt;, all indicating dangerously increased intracranial pressure.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Bartholin&amp;rsquo;s gland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The symptoms described &amp;mdash; a &lt;strong&gt;painful, tender swelling&lt;/strong&gt; on one side of the vaginal opening, with discomfort while sitting or walking &amp;mdash; are classic signs of a &lt;strong&gt;Bartholin&amp;rsquo;s gland infection or Bartholin cyst/abscess&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Bartholin&amp;rsquo;s glands are located at the &lt;strong&gt;posterior (lower) part of the vaginal opening&lt;/strong&gt;, one on each side. They normally secrete mucus to lubricate the vaginal opening.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;When the duct becomes blocked, mucus accumulates, forming a cyst. If infected (commonly by &lt;strong&gt;E. coli&lt;/strong&gt; or sexually transmitted bacteria), it turns into a &lt;strong&gt;painful abscess&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pain increases with activities such as &lt;strong&gt;walking, sitting, or sexual intercourse&lt;/strong&gt;, and swelling is easily felt externally.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Thus, the presentation strongly indicates an infection of the &lt;strong&gt;Bartholin&amp;rsquo;s gland&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Skene&amp;rsquo;s gland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Skene&amp;rsquo;s glands are located near the &lt;strong&gt;urethral opening&lt;/strong&gt;. Infection here causes pain near the urethra or difficulty urinating&amp;mdash;not swelling near the vaginal opening.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Sebaceous gland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Sebaceous cysts can occur anywhere on the skin but typically present as &lt;strong&gt;non-tender&lt;/strong&gt; lumps&amp;mdash;not painful, deep swellings at the vaginal opening.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Apocrine gland&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Apocrine gland infections (hidradenitis) occur in the groin or axilla but appear as &lt;strong&gt;boils or nodules&lt;/strong&gt;, not specifically at the vaginal opening.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A unilateral, painful swelling at the vaginal opening is most characteristic of a &lt;strong&gt;Bartholin&amp;rsquo;s gland infection or abscess&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct diagnosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Non-reactive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A Non-Stress Test (NST) evaluates fetal well-being by assessing &lt;strong&gt;fetal heart rate accelerations&lt;/strong&gt; in response to fetal movements. A healthy fetus should show adequate accelerations that reflect good oxygenation and neurological function.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;For pregnancies at or beyond &lt;strong&gt;32 weeks&lt;/strong&gt;, a **reactive NST** must include: &lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;At least &lt;strong&gt;2 accelerations&lt;/strong&gt; in 20 minutes&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Each acceleration &amp;ge; &lt;strong&gt;15 bpm above baseline&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Each lasting at least &lt;strong&gt;15 seconds&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;In this case, the NST shows: &lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Baseline: &lt;strong&gt;135 bpm&lt;/strong&gt; (normal)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Minimal variability&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;mdash; a concerning sign suggesting reduced fetal oxygenation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Only &lt;strong&gt;one small acceleration&lt;/strong&gt;: 10 bpm &amp;times; 10 sec (does NOT meet 15&amp;times;15 criteria)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since the NST &lt;strong&gt;does not meet the criteria for reactivity&lt;/strong&gt; (only one inadequate acceleration, minimal variability), the strip is classified as a &lt;strong&gt;non-reactive NST&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A non-reactive NST indicates that the fetus may not be adequately oxygenated, and further evaluation (like BPP or CST) is required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Reactive&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: A reactive NST requires at least two accelerations of 15&amp;times;15. This tracing has only one and does not meet criteria.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Sinusoidal pattern&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: A sinusoidal tracing is smooth, wavelike, and lacks variability. The NST here shows &lt;strong&gt;minimal variability&lt;/strong&gt;, but not a sinusoidal rhythm.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Positive test&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: &amp;ldquo;Positive&amp;rdquo; is used for a &lt;strong&gt;Contraction Stress Test (CST)&lt;/strong&gt;, not an NST. NSTs are interpreted only as reactive or non-reactive.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The fetal tracing lacks adequate accelerations and shows minimal variability, making this NST clearly &lt;strong&gt;non-reactive&lt;/strong&gt; and requiring further assessment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Ventricular repolarization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;T wave&lt;/strong&gt; on a normal ECG represents &lt;strong&gt;ventricular repolarization&lt;/strong&gt;, which is the electrical recovery phase of the ventricles after they contract.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Repolarization allows the ventricles to reset electrically so they can be ready for the next heartbeat.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This process occurs after the QRS complex, which represents ventricular depolarization (contraction). The T wave reflects the ventricles returning to a resting electrical state.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;T wave abnormalities (tall, peaked, inverted, or flattened T waves) can indicate conditions such as &lt;strong&gt;hyperkalemia, ischemia, myocardial infarction, or electrolyte imbalances&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since the question asks specifically about the T wave, the correct interpretation is &lt;strong&gt;ventricular repolarization&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Atrial depolarization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Atrial depolarization is shown by the &lt;strong&gt;P wave&lt;/strong&gt;, not the T wave.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Atrial repolarization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Atrial repolarization occurs but is &lt;strong&gt;not visible&lt;/strong&gt; on the ECG because it is hidden within the QRS complex.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Ventricular depolarization&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Ventricular depolarization is represented by the &lt;strong&gt;QRS complex&lt;/strong&gt;, not the T wave.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The T wave corresponds to &lt;strong&gt;ventricular repolarization&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Bradycardia with low blood pressure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A potassium level of &lt;strong&gt;6.2 mEq/L&lt;/strong&gt; with &lt;strong&gt;peaked T waves&lt;/strong&gt; on ECG indicates serious &lt;strong&gt;hyperkalemia&lt;/strong&gt;, which can cause life-threatening arrhythmias. To stabilize the heart, &lt;strong&gt;IV calcium gluconate&lt;/strong&gt; is administered.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Calcium gluconate works by &lt;strong&gt;stabilizing the myocardial cell membrane&lt;/strong&gt; but does not lower potassium. It must be administered slowly while monitoring cardiac rhythm.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The major risk during IV calcium infusion is &lt;strong&gt;cardiac conduction disturbances&lt;/strong&gt;, especially &lt;strong&gt;bradycardia&lt;/strong&gt;, heart blocks, or even asystole if given too rapidly.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Calcium can also cause &lt;strong&gt;vasodilation&lt;/strong&gt; and myocardial effects that may lead to &lt;strong&gt;hypotension&lt;/strong&gt;, especially in patients with unstable cardiac rhythms.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Therefore, during infusion, the nurse must watch closely for &lt;strong&gt;bradycardia and dropping blood pressure&lt;/strong&gt;, as these indicate cardiovascular depression and require immediate intervention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Severe increase in blood pressure&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: IV calcium does not typically cause hypertension; if anything, it can produce mild hypotension due to vasodilation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Low blood sugar&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Calcium has no effect on glucose metabolism. Hypoglycemia is unrelated to calcium gluconate infusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Slowed breathing&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Respiratory depression is not a known complication of IV calcium. It is more associated with opioid overdose or sedative medications.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Because calcium gluconate affects cardiac conduction, the nurse must monitor for &lt;strong&gt;bradycardia with hypotension&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;the most important complication during infusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: He said that he was busy then.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;When converting from &lt;strong&gt;direct to indirect speech&lt;/strong&gt;, changes in pronouns, tense, and time expressions are required.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The reporting verb &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;said&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; is in the past tense, so the verb inside the quotation must also shift from present to past:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;am&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;rarr; &lt;strong&gt;was&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Time expressions also change:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;now&lt;/strong&gt; &amp;rarr; &lt;strong&gt;then&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Pronoun &amp;ldquo;I&amp;rdquo; changes to &amp;ldquo;he&amp;rdquo; according to the subject of the reporting verb.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Thus, the correct indirect form is: &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;He said that he was busy then.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;He said that I am busy now.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Incorrect tense and pronoun; direct speech is not converted.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;He told that he is busy.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Incorrect tense and incorrect reporting verb usage (&amp;ldquo;told&amp;rdquo; requires an object).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;He said he was busy now.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Time expression not converted (&amp;ldquo;now&amp;rdquo; should become &amp;ldquo;then&amp;rdquo;).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The grammatically correct indirect speech conversion is: &lt;strong&gt;&amp;ldquo;He said that he was busy then.&amp;rdquo;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Canada&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Canada has the &lt;strong&gt;longest total coastline in the world&lt;/strong&gt;, stretching over approximately &lt;strong&gt;202,000 kilometers&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This is because Canada has numerous &lt;strong&gt;bays, inlets, islands, and Arctic coastlines&lt;/strong&gt; that greatly extend its total shore length.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Its coastline borders three major oceans: &lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Atlantic Ocean&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Pacific Ocean&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Arctic Ocean&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This vast coastal geography makes Canada the undisputed leader in terms of total coastline length.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Australia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Australia has a long coastline, but it is much shorter than Canada&amp;rsquo;s.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Indonesia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Indonesia, being an archipelago, has a very long coastline but still &lt;strong&gt;does not exceed Canada&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Russia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Russia has an extensive Arctic and Pacific coastline but ranks below Canada in total length.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Canada has the &lt;strong&gt;longest coastline in the world&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: 9 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The prescribed dose is &lt;strong&gt;15 mcg/min&lt;/strong&gt; of nitroglycerin by continuous IV infusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The IV solution available contains &lt;strong&gt;50 mg in 500 mL&lt;/strong&gt; of D5W.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;First, convert the drug in the bag from mg to mcg:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;1 mg = 1,000 mcg&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;50 mg = 50 &amp;times; 1,000 = &lt;strong&gt;50,000 mcg&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Now calculate the concentration of the solution:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;50,000 mcg in 500 mL &amp;rarr; Concentration = 50,000 mcg &amp;divide; 500 mL = &lt;strong&gt;100 mcg/mL&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Next, convert the ordered dose from mcg/min to mcg/hr (because the pump is set in mL/hr):&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;15 mcg/min &amp;times; 60 min/hr = &lt;strong&gt;900 mcg/hr&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Now use the formula: &lt;strong&gt;mL/hr = (required dose per hour in mcg/hr) &amp;divide; (concentration in mcg/mL)&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;mL/hr = 900 mcg/hr &amp;divide; 100 mcg/mL = &lt;strong&gt;9 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Therefore, to deliver 15 mcg/min from this solution, the infusion pump must be set at &lt;strong&gt;9 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;3 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: 3 mL/hr would provide only 300 mcg/hr (3 &amp;times; 100), which equals 5 mcg/min&amp;mdash;too low compared to the ordered 15 mcg/min.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;6 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: 6 mL/hr would deliver 600 mcg/hr (6 &amp;times; 100), equal to 10 mcg/min&amp;mdash;still below the prescribed dose.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;15 mL/hr&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: 15 mL/hr would give 1,500 mcg/hr (15 &amp;times; 100), which equals 25 mcg/min&amp;mdash;this exceeds the ordered dose and could cause hypotension.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Using the given concentration (100 mcg/mL) and the prescribed rate (15 mcg/min), the correct IV pump setting is &lt;strong&gt;9 mL/hr&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the accurate and safe choice.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: 10 units&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A &amp;ldquo;&lt;strong&gt;massive transfusion&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;rdquo; is defined in adults as the replacement of an amount of blood roughly equal to the patient&amp;rsquo;s &lt;strong&gt;total blood volume within 24 hours&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The average adult blood volume is approximately &lt;strong&gt;70 mL per kg&lt;/strong&gt;. For a 70-kg adult, this equals about &lt;strong&gt;5,000 mL (5 liters)&lt;/strong&gt; of blood.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;One unit of packed red blood cells (PRBCs) contains about &lt;strong&gt;300&amp;ndash;350 mL&lt;/strong&gt; of blood product.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Replacing 5 liters of blood would therefore require around: &lt;strong&gt;5,000 mL &amp;divide; 350 mL &amp;asymp; 10&amp;ndash;14 units&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Clinically, massive transfusion is standardized as &lt;strong&gt;10 or more units of PRBCs in 24 hours&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This volume triggers the need for a &lt;strong&gt;massive transfusion protocol (MTP)&lt;/strong&gt;, which includes balanced replacement of RBCs, plasma, and platelets.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;3 units&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Far below the volume of an adult&amp;rsquo;s blood; does not approach massive transfusion levels.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;5 units&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Represents only about half a normal adult blood volume; not considered massive transfusion.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;20 units&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Extremely high and not part of the standard definition, though some severe trauma cases may exceed 20 units.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Massive transfusion in adults is defined as giving &lt;strong&gt;10 units of PRBCs within 24 hours&lt;/strong&gt;, which approximates a full blood volume replacement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Russia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Sputnik V&lt;/strong&gt; was the world&amp;rsquo;s first registered COVID-19 vaccine, developed in &lt;strong&gt;Russia&lt;/strong&gt; by the &lt;strong&gt;Gamaleya Research Institute of Epidemiology and Microbiology&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The vaccine uses a &lt;strong&gt;viral vector platform&lt;/strong&gt; with two different adenoviruses (Ad26 and Ad5) administered in two doses to produce a strong immune response.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;It was named &amp;ldquo;Sputnik V&amp;rdquo; as a reference to the historic Russian satellite &amp;ldquo;Sputnik,&amp;rdquo; symbolizing scientific achievement.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Russia approved the vaccine for emergency use in August 2020, making it the first COVID-19 vaccine to be officially registered.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Thus, the vaccine&amp;rsquo;s origin is clearly &lt;strong&gt;Russia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;China&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: China developed vaccines like &lt;strong&gt;Sinovac&lt;/strong&gt; and &lt;strong&gt;Sinopharm&lt;/strong&gt;, not Sputnik V.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;United Kingdom&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: The UK developed the &lt;strong&gt;Oxford&amp;ndash;AstraZeneca&lt;/strong&gt; vaccine, not Sputnik V.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;United States&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: The U.S. developed &lt;strong&gt;Pfizer&amp;ndash;BioNTech&lt;/strong&gt; (with Germany) and &lt;strong&gt;Moderna&lt;/strong&gt; vaccines.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;Sputnik V&lt;/strong&gt; COVID-19 vaccine was originally developed in &lt;strong&gt;Russia&lt;/strong&gt;, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Measles&amp;ndash;Mumps&amp;ndash;Rubella (MMR)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Live attenuated vaccines&lt;/strong&gt; contain weakened (attenuated) forms of the pathogens that can still replicate but do not cause disease in healthy individuals.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The &lt;strong&gt;MMR vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; (Measles&amp;ndash;Mumps&amp;ndash;Rubella) is a classic example of a &lt;strong&gt;live attenuated vaccine&lt;/strong&gt;. Each component&amp;mdash;measles, mumps, and rubella viruses&amp;mdash;is weakened so the immune system can mount a strong, long-lasting response.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Live attenuated vaccines usually produce &lt;strong&gt;excellent lifelong immunity&lt;/strong&gt; with fewer doses because they closely mimic natural infection.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;These vaccines are NOT given to severely immunocompromised individuals or in pregnancy due to the risk of uncontrolled viral replication.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Therefore, among the given choices, &lt;strong&gt;MMR&lt;/strong&gt; is the correct example of a live attenuated vaccine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Inactivated Polio Vaccine (IPV)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: IPV contains &lt;strong&gt;killed (inactivated)&lt;/strong&gt; poliovirus. It cannot replicate, so it is not a live vaccine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Hepatitis B vaccine&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Hepatitis B vaccine is a &lt;strong&gt;recombinant subunit vaccine&lt;/strong&gt; made using HBsAg protein, not a live virus.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Tetanus toxoid (TT)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: TT is a &lt;strong&gt;toxoid vaccine&lt;/strong&gt;, made from inactivated toxins of *Clostridium tetani*, not a live organism.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;MMR&lt;/strong&gt; is the only option that consists of live attenuated viruses, making it the correct answer.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Perseveration&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;During a mental status examination, when a patient &lt;strong&gt;repeats the same words, phrases, or ideas over and over&lt;/strong&gt; regardless of the question asked, this pattern is called &lt;strong&gt;perseveration&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Perseveration reflects a disturbance in &lt;strong&gt;executive functioning&lt;/strong&gt; and the brain&amp;rsquo;s ability to shift thoughts. It is commonly seen in conditions such as &lt;strong&gt;frontal lobe disorders, dementia, traumatic brain injury, and schizophrenia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The key feature is that the patient keeps repeating their own previous response, not someone else&amp;rsquo;s words. The repetition occurs &lt;strong&gt;inappropriately and involuntarily&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This repetition persists even when questions change, showing a lack of cognitive flexibility and impaired mental control.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Because the patient continues repeating the same phrase regardless of stimulus or question, the behavior is accurately described as &lt;strong&gt;perseveration&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Echolalia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Echolalia is the repetition of &lt;strong&gt;another person&amp;rsquo;s words&lt;/strong&gt;, not one&amp;rsquo;s own. It is common in autism and schizophrenia. This does not match the scenario.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Clang association&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Clang associations involve speech driven by &lt;strong&gt;rhyming or similar sounds&lt;/strong&gt;, not repetition of the same phrase.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Word salad&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Word salad is a completely disorganized mixture of words with no logical connection. It is not repetitive speech.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since the patient continuously repeats the same phrase despite different questions, the most accurate diagnosis is &lt;strong&gt;perseveration&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Cardiovascular system&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A normal umbilical cord contains &lt;strong&gt;two arteries and one vein&lt;/strong&gt;. A **single umbilical artery (SUA)** is the most common umbilical cord anomaly.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;SUA is strongly associated with a higher risk of **congenital anomalies**, most commonly involving the: &lt;strong&gt;genitourinary system (kidneys)&lt;/strong&gt; and the &lt;strong&gt;cardiovascular system&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Among the answer choices provided, the **cardiovascular system** is the most frequently associated system.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Common cardiac abnormalities seen with SUA include: &lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Ventricular septal defect (VSD)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Patent ductus arteriosus (PDA)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;span&gt;Complex congenital heart defects&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;span&gt; &lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Because cardiovascular anomalies occur more often than abnormalities in the other listed systems, the correct choice is the **cardiovascular system**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Respiratory system&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: SUA is not commonly linked with primary lung abnormalities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Musculoskeletal system&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Though some limb anomalies can occur, they are less common than cardiovascular defects.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Neurological system&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: CNS anomalies can occur but are not the most frequent association.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Among the listed choices, congenital defects associated with a single umbilical artery most commonly involve the &lt;strong&gt;cardiovascular system&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The ABG values show a high pH (7.55), meaning the blood is alkalotic.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The PaCO2 is low (24 mmHg). Since CO2 acts as an acid, a low PaCO2 makes the blood more alkaline, indicating a respiratory cause.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The HCO3 level is normal (22 mEq/L), meaning the kidneys have not begun compensating yet. In compensation, bicarbonate would decrease, but here it remains unchanged.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This pattern of high pH, low PaCO2, and normal HCO3 is characteristic of uncompensated respiratory alkalosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;In this scenario, the panic attack leads to hyperventilation, causing excessive loss of CO2 and resulting in respiratory alkalosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Metabolic alkalosis, partially compensated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: In metabolic alkalosis, HCO3 would be high, not normal. Additionally, PaCO2 would increase to compensate, not decrease.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Metabolic acidosis, uncompensated&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Metabolic acidosis would show low pH and low HCO3. Here the pH is high, not low.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Compensated respiratory acidosis&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Respiratory acidosis would have increased PaCO2, not decreased. pH would also be normal if compensated.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The ABG values (alkalotic pH, low PaCO2, normal HCO3) clearly indicate uncompensated respiratory alkalosis, commonly seen during hyperventilation in a panic attack.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: XBUDI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The pattern used in the coding of CLOCK &amp;rarr; DMPEL is a simple alphabetical shift.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Each letter in the original word is shifted forward by one position in the alphabet:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;C &amp;rarr; D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;L &amp;rarr; M&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;O &amp;rarr; P&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;C &amp;rarr; D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;K &amp;rarr; L&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Applying the same +1 alphabetical shift to WATCH:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;W &amp;rarr; X&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A &amp;rarr; B&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;T &amp;rarr; U&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;C &amp;rarr; D&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;H &amp;rarr; I&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Thus, WATCH becomes XBUDI.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;XBXDI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Incorrect shifts; letters do not follow the +1 pattern consistently.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;YBUDI&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: First letter incorrectly shifted by +2 instead of +1.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;XBUDJ&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Last letter incorrectly shifted (H &amp;rarr; I, not J).&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The correct coding pattern is a +1 shift for every letter, and the accurate coded word is XBUDI.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: eSanjeevani &amp;ndash; Telemedicine Service&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The national telemedicine helpline **14567** is part of **eSanjeevani**, India&amp;rsquo;s government-supported telemedicine platform.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This service provides citizens with **remote medical consultations**, including:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Doctor-to-patient teleconsultation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Medical advice&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Digital prescription generation&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;It is especially important for people in **rural and remote areas** who cannot easily access health facilities.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The scenario describes the patient calling 14567, getting advice and a prescription&amp;mdash;all of which fall under **eSanjeevani Telemedicine Service**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Nikshay Poshan Yojana&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: This scheme provides nutritional support to tuberculosis patients, not telemedicine services.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Ayushman Bharat &amp;ndash; PMJAY&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: PMJAY offers cashless treatment for hospitalized care, not teleconsultations.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Janani Shishu Suraksha Karyakram (JSSK)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: JSSK provides free maternal and newborn health services, not telemedicine.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The telemedicine call to 14567 for a remote consultation and prescription clearly falls under **eSanjeevani &amp;ndash; Telemedicine Service**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Hyperkalemia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;In respiratory acidosis, the lungs cannot remove enough carbon dioxide, leading to an increase in PaCO2 and a fall in blood pH.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Acidosis causes hydrogen ions (H+) to move into the cells. To maintain electrical balance, potassium ions (K+) move out of the cells into the bloodstream.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This shift of potassium from intracellular to extracellular space results in a high serum potassium level, known as hyperkalemia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;COPD exacerbations commonly produce CO2 retention and acute respiratory acidosis. As the acidosis worsens, potassium rises, making hyperkalemia a frequent electrolyte abnormality to monitor.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Because respiratory acidosis drives K+ out of cells, the most expected imbalance is hyperkalemia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Hyponatremia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Sodium levels are not typically affected by respiratory acidosis. Hyponatremia is more associated with fluid imbalance or endocrine issues.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Hypokalemia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: This is the opposite of what occurs in acidosis. Hypokalemia happens in alkalosis, not acidosis.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Hypercalcemia&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Calcium levels do not significantly change during respiratory acidosis and are unrelated to CO2 retention.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;In respiratory acidosis, particularly in COPD flare-ups, potassium moves out of cells into the blood, making hyperkalemia the most common electrolyte abnormality.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Abruptio placentae&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The woman presents with &lt;strong&gt;severe constant abdominal pain&lt;/strong&gt;, &lt;strong&gt;dark red vaginal bleeding&lt;/strong&gt;, and a &lt;strong&gt;firm, tender uterus&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This combination of symptoms is classic for &lt;strong&gt;abruptio placentae&lt;/strong&gt;, a condition where the placenta prematurely separates from the uterine wall.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Abruption causes accumulation of blood behind the placenta, leading to:&lt;/span&gt;
	&lt;ul&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;painful bleeding (dark red)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;uterine rigidity (&amp;ldquo;board-like uterus&amp;rdquo;)&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
		&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;uterine tenderness&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;/ul&gt;
	&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Twin pregnancy is a known risk factor because it overstretches the uterus, increasing the chance of placental detachment.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since the clinical picture strongly matches these features, the most likely diagnosis is abruptio placentae.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Placenta previa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Previa causes &lt;strong&gt;painless bright red bleeding&lt;/strong&gt; with a soft uterus. The scenario describes painful bleeding and a firm uterus, ruling it out.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Vasa previa&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: In vasa previa, fetal vessels rupture, causing &lt;strong&gt;sudden painless bleeding&lt;/strong&gt; at membrane rupture. It does not cause uterine tenderness or firmness.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Preterm labor&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Labor pain is intermittent and rhythmic, not constant; uterine tenderness and dark bleeding are not typical features.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The presence of constant abdominal pain, dark bleeding, and a firm tender uterus most accurately indicates &lt;strong&gt;abruptio placentae&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Eclampsia; start IV magnesium sulfate and get ready for delivery&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The woman has experienced a &lt;strong&gt;tonic&amp;ndash;clonic seizure&lt;/strong&gt; during pregnancy. A seizure in a pregnant woman with hypertension and proteinuria is the defining feature of &lt;strong&gt;eclampsia&lt;/strong&gt;.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Her blood pressure is severely elevated at &lt;strong&gt;160/110 mmHg&lt;/strong&gt;, and urine shows &lt;strong&gt;3+ protein&lt;/strong&gt;&amp;mdash;both classic findings of severe pre-eclampsia progressing to eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The immediate and most important treatment step is to start &lt;strong&gt;IV magnesium sulfate&lt;/strong&gt;, which is the drug of choice for controlling and preventing seizures in eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Once seizures are controlled and the mother stabilized, the next priority is to plan for &lt;strong&gt;delivery&lt;/strong&gt;, as delivery is the definitive management for eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Thus, the correct diagnosis is eclampsia, and the priority intervention is starting magnesium sulfate followed by preparation for delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Severe pre-eclampsia; give antihypertensives and observe&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: This is incorrect because the presence of a seizure means the patient already progressed to &lt;strong&gt;eclampsia&lt;/strong&gt;, not just severe pre-eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;HELLP syndrome; give steroids for lung maturity&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: HELLP includes hemolysis, elevated liver enzymes, and low platelets. None of these findings are mentioned. Seizures point more clearly to eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Gestational hypertension; strict rest and NST&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Gestational hypertension has no proteinuria or seizures. The patient has both, confirming eclampsia.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;A tonic&amp;ndash;clonic seizure in a hypertensive pregnant woman with proteinuria indicates &lt;strong&gt;eclampsia&lt;/strong&gt;, and the priority treatment is &lt;strong&gt;IV magnesium sulfate&lt;/strong&gt; with preparation for delivery.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Correct Answer: Public health surveillance&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Rationale&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The process described involves **regular, continuous collection, analysis, interpretation, and dissemination of health data**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;This ongoing and systematic activity is known as **public health surveillance**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Surveillance helps health authorities identify trends, detect outbreaks early, and plan or evaluate public health programs effectively.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;It is a cornerstone of public health practice because it provides **real-time information** for decision making, priority setting, and resource allocation.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Since the question emphasizes an organized, routine, and continuous process to support program planning, the correct term is public health surveillance.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;u&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Explanation of Other Options:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/u&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Epidemiology&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Epidemiology is the broader science of studying disease distribution and determinants; it is not limited to continuous data monitoring.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Randomized controlled trial&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: RCTs are experimental research studies to test interventions, not routine health data monitoring.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;strong&gt;&lt;span&gt;Retrospective study&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/strong&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;Rationale: Retrospective studies analyze past data; they are not ongoing surveillance systems.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;

&lt;div&gt;&lt;span&gt;&lt;strong&gt;Conclusion&lt;/strong&gt;:&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/div&gt;

&lt;ul&gt;
	&lt;li&gt;&lt;span&gt;The continuous and systematic monitoring of health information for planning programs is known as **public health surveillance**.&lt;/span&gt;&lt;/li&gt;
&lt;/ul&gt;
